<a id="source-ceece64828e8"></a>

<a id="source-ceece64828e8-sysml-style-specification-review"></a>

# SysML-style specification review

The review used the official **OMG SysML 2.0, Part 1: Language Specification**, document formal/2026-03-02, March 2026. Section 7 presents representative element/graphical/textual notation tables; section 8.2.3 defines graphical notation in more detail. These serve complementary reading purposes. [Official language PDF](https://www.omg.org/spec/SysML/2.0/Language/PDF).

| Reviewed clause | Presentation concern | SWAML treatment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 8.2.3.5 | Namespaces and packages | namespace frame and explicit import reference |
| 8.2.3.6 | Definition and usage | mandatory def distinction and occurrence typing |
| 8.2.3.12â€“14 | Ports, connections, interfaces | boundary squares, nominal contracts, explicit routes |
| 8.2.3.17 | Actions | owned function flow only |
| 8.2.3.18 | States | flat states directly owned by a function |
| 8.2.3.21 | Requirements | prose obligations and claimed coverage |
| 8.2.3.26 | Views and viewpoints | existing SWAML view families |

Visual inspection included printed pages 20, 199, 210 and 232. It confirmed the separation of representative tables from graphical productions and the use of explicit headers and compartments. This is a review of organization, not evidence of language compatibility.

SWAML's [normative notation](#source-5798f00f85a6) and [canonical catalog](#source-2d93a6cc8eab) are original designs. Their coverage includes every grammar production and keyword, while distinguishing glyphs, compartments, labels and text-only syntax. Each concept has reproducible SVG/PNG examples and contextual constraints. No OMG figure or text is copied into those assets. SWAML does not add performers, free actions/events, hierarchical states or other unsupported constructs to resemble another language's diagrams.

---

<a id="source-237cbcd491f2"></a>

<a id="source-237cbcd491f2-5-kerml-semantic-mapping"></a>

# 5. KerML semantic mapping

Normative SWAML mapping contract, with implementation status stated explicitly. Part of the [SWAML 1.3 specification](#source-ffe5ff67da37).

<a id="source-237cbcd491f2-51-foundation-and-boundary"></a>

## 5.1 Foundation and boundary

SWAML defines software-specific specializations and constraints over a normalized KerML model. Its concrete syntax is independent: a `.swaml` file is not automatically valid `.kerml` text. The Langium AST is an authoring representation, not itself the KerML abstract syntax.

The selected foundation is [OMG KerML 1.0](https://www.omg.org/spec/KerML/1.0). KerML supplies structural classifiers/features, behavior classifiers/steps, connectors, successions and expressions. The relevant normative kernel concepts are in clauses 7.3â€“7.4 and their abstract-syntax/semantic counterparts. SWAML's mapping below is this project's proposed specialization contract, not an OMG-approved extension. [KerML specification](https://www.omg.org/spec/KerML/1.0/PDF).

The [mapping inventory](../spec/kerml-mapping.json) is a machine-readable design contract. This release does not implement or validate a KerML exporter, does not bundle a kernel semantic library, and does not claim K conformance. Implementers must test the normalized representation against the selected KerML metamodel and library before making that claim.

<a id="source-237cbcd491f2-52-semantic-specializations"></a>

## 5.2 Semantic specializations

| SWAML element | Proposed KerML base/representation | SWAML restriction or additional meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Package | Package | flat authoring namespace |
| Class definition | Structure | composition, encapsulated state and local executable features; optional single class specialization |
| Component definition | SWAML Class specialization over Structure | preserves Component as the specialized class kind; may specialize a class or component definition |
| Specialization / redefinition | Specialization / Redefinition | explicit source reuse with SWAML compatibility checks and provenance |
| Class/component execution root | composite Feature typed by a referenced or synthetic Structure | selected named occurrence, closed topology and effective execution profile; typed binding cannot extend topology |
| Class/component usage | composite Feature typed by its Structure | declared usage kind, compatible definition kind, exactly-one multiplicity and acyclic composition |
| Value datatype / enum | DataType | finite value semantics; enum domain restriction |
| Interface contract | Classifier | nominal version, legal members and communication mode |
| Port | Feature typed by contract Classifier | direction/role projection and boundary ownership |
| Operation/function signature | Behavior plus typed parameter Features and preserved callable kind | no implementation; Function specializes Operation; copied request/result contract |
| Operation implementation | Behavior and an owned executable Step typed by it | direct class/component ownership, activation, serialization and timing |
| Function implementation | SWAML Operation specialization over Behavior | preserves specialized Function kind with the same execution rules |
| Calculation | Function | parameter-only environment and no side effects |
| QoS policy definition | named MetadataFeature with typed policy fields | reusable values and reference provenance; never shared queue state |
| Connector | Connector between port Features | permitted interface exchange and QoS obligations |
| Delegation | Connector plus identity-preserving forwarding constraint | same role and contract; no extra queue |
| Ordered flow | contained Steps and Successions | structured branches/loops, bounded execution |
| Message emission | Step typed by a SWAML-library communication Behavior | source activation, route and correlation metadata |
| State / transition | contained behavior steps, state predicate and succession relations | SWAML state-selection and reaction semantics |
| Requirement / case / view | immutable attributes, formal docs, directed satisfy/verify memberships and selected occurrence identities | SWAML-specific interpretation |

These SWAML subtype relations MUST be retained even when the authoring AST shares a representation between the two structural kinds or between the two executable kinds. A SWAML executable function is not mapped to KerML Function solely because of its spelling: the pure calculation row uses KerML Function, while operations/functions use the constrained executable Behavior mapping.

The table establishes a mapping target, not a claim that names such as â€œSWAML stateâ€ already exist as native KerML metaclasses. A full exporter needs a versioned SWAML semantic library for the additional predicates, activation and communication constraints. [Chapter 7](#source-0db79669e756) completes the information model, reference edges and lowering rules for every syntax family; the inventory covers every current grammar rule/type name.

<a id="source-237cbcd491f2-53-normalization-algorithm"></a>

## 5.3 Normalization algorithm

A mapper MUST perform the following in dependency order:

1. Validate the complete workspace and resolve every textual reference. Allocate stable IDs for package declarations and each named semantic element. Retain file URI and source range as provenance, not as identity.
2. Create value types and interface classifiers. Materialize every data/event/function parameter and result as a typed feature with copied-value semantics. Preserve interface version and member order.
3. Create class/component structures and owned usage features, preserving the declared structural kind and Component-to-Class subtype relation, plus synthetic structures for inline root composition and behavior, with membership and typing links. Each root is a named occurrence of its referenced or synthetic structure; a typed root has no extra wrapper child. Preserve source profile omission in provenance and effective service in semantics. Keep definition identity separate from every instance-path identity. A reused definition must not merge the states of its occurrences.
4. Create each port feature in its owning structure. Apply role-specific direction to each member projection: RPC inputs/results have opposite directions; service events run provider-to-consumer. Do not assign a single simplistic â€œoutâ€ bit to an entire bidirectional service contract.
5. Create operation/function behaviors within their class/component ownership, preserving the declared executable kind and Function-to-Operation subtype relation. Add a synthetic owned executable step typed by that behavior and a component-instance context binding. This lowering artifact gives execution a structural context; it is not a user-visible performer or a second editable allocation model.
6. Lower statement blocks to structured steps with explicit sequencing. A branch has a Boolean selection predicate and branch result control; a bounded loop has condition, body, count and failure edge. Lower expressions as typed expressions/calculations. A mapper must preserve source order and short-circuit evaluation.
7. Lower each stateful function to one state identity feature per activation, nested state-entry/exit/effect behaviors, trigger predicates, priority selection and succession relationships. Preserve finality, timer start/cancel and the run-to-completion relation. A bare succession graph without these constraints is not an equivalent mapping.
8. Lower communication statements to message occurrences linked to the emitting activation, sending port, interface member, connector/delegation route and destination port. Requests additionally carry correlation, timeout and response/error continuation. An event cannot become an unowned signal merely because a kernel target permits it.
9. Materialize queues, policy constraints, timing/deployment annotations and case happens-before relations in the SWAML semantic library vocabulary. Keep queue identity per connector/direction/member.
10. Produce a reverse mapping from every generated element to its authoring element, including synthetic lowering elements. Validate kernel typing/membership constraints and SWAML constraints separately. Report failures at the source element, not only at generated IDs.

<a id="source-237cbcd491f2-54-required-extension-predicates"></a>

## 5.4 Required extension predicates

The semantic library MUST define, or a mapper MUST carry as explicit constrained metadata, at least:

- `ownsExecutable(classOrComponent, operationOrFunction)` and instance-local `contextOf(activation)`;
- `contractOf(port)`, `roleOf(port)`, `memberOf(contract, member)`;
- `route(sourcePort, destinationPort, connectorPath)`;
- `emits(activation, port, member, copiedPayload)` and `handles(function, port, member)`;
- `correlates(request, response)`, `expiresAt(request)` and `lateResponseDiscarded(response)`;
- `queueOf(connector, direction, member)`, capacity, overflow, ordering and delivery policy;
- `currentState(activation)`, `enabled(transition, stimulus)`, priority and final-state completion;
- `wcet(function)`, `deadline(function)`, `period(function)` and instance allocation;
- `happensBefore(caseStepA, caseStepB)`, `satisfy(designElement, requirement)` and `verify(verificationCase, requirement)`.

These predicates are part of SWAML meaning even if an exchange format stores them as metadata. A consumer lacking their interpretation MUST report a partial import and MUST NOT execute a â€œbest guess.â€ A generic KerML consumer can preserve structural content while lacking SWAML runtime semantics.

<a id="source-237cbcd491f2-55-interchange-and-round-trip-obligations"></a>

## 5.5 Interchange and round-trip obligations

A future exporter SHOULD use the standard KerML interchange representation supported by its chosen backend, with a declared SWAML semantic library version. No new unregistered MIME type, XMI metamodel URI, or fake OMG namespace is assigned by this document. SWAML library identifiers must use a project-controlled namespace when published.

Round-trip tests MUST compare semantic identities, typing, owned instances, routes, triggers, guards, flow ordering, queue policies, timing and trace dependencies. byte-for-byte textual equality is not required. Comments, formatting and diagram layouts are separate preservation requirements for an authoring tool.

Dropping a guard, converting a queued event to a sampled value, merging per-subscriber queues, or omitting a request's response path is a semantic loss and must be reported as an error for K conformance. The mapping must not infer a performer allocation that changes the function's direct owning component.

---

<a id="source-0db79669e756"></a>

<a id="source-0db79669e756-7-normalized-semantic-model"></a>

# 7. Normalized semantic model

Normative SWAML contract extending [chapter 5](#source-237cbcd491f2). This chapter defines the complete information a mapper must preserve. It specifies SWAML normalization, not a new OMG metamodel or an implemented serializer.

The semantic hierarchy includes **Component specializes Class** and **Function specializes Operation**. Shared AST productions do not erase these kinds. The normalization rules below apply to both structural and executable alternatives; an interface signature remains a contract signature rather than an executable declaration.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-71-identity-ownership-and-source-trace"></a>

## 7.1 Identity, ownership and source trace

Normalization consumes a resolved workspace and emits a finite declaration graph plus instance expansions for selected root components. Each semantic node has an immutable identity within that normalized artifact, a semantic kind, at most one owning node, ordered owned children where order matters, typed reference edges, and source provenance. Except for model roots, every semantic node has exactly one owner. Reference edges never imply containment.

The deterministic textual identity of a declaration is its package plus owned-name path, for example `Settings::SettingStore.update`. Anonymous syntax nodes use the owning declaration ID plus a structural slot path such as `/body/statements/2/condition`; these IDs are stable for an unchanged syntax tree, not across arbitrary edits. Synthetic elements append a reserved role segment such as `/@execution` or `/@failure`. No source identifier contains `/` or `@`, so generated roles cannot collide with source names.

An occurrence identity consists of the root component declaration ID and the child-instance path. A port occurrence adds the owned port name. A function occurrence additionally refers to its shared function declaration and component occurrence. Runtime activations add a run ID and unique activation ordinal. A mapper MUST NOT collapse occurrence paths merely because they share a definition. Persistent editor UUIDs are a separate identity map with explicit rename/move history; source locations and in-memory AST addresses are never persistent IDs.

A reverse trace relation maps every generated element to one or more authoring node identities and their URI/ranges. Synthetic nodes retain their source owner and generation role. No generated constraint may be untraceable. An import can have an import-declaration source location while referencing a package located in another document.

An authored or generated test driver retains the same component/function ownership and source provenance as application behavior. Its event declarations, publisher ports, connectors, schedules and receiving member bindings are normalized explicitly; no hidden harness activation is added.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-72-declaration-graph-and-links"></a>

## 7.2 Declaration graph and links

| Normalized family | Required information and constraints |
| --- | --- |
| Workspace/package | language version, packages, direct import edges, unique declaration namespaces |
| Record/enum | nominal identity, ordered fields or ordered unique literal names, finite domain/containment |
| Type use | primitive or nominal target, optional array extent; never encode a type solely by a display label |
| Interface | kind, version, ordered data/event/RPC members, operation/function signature kind, data mode and idempotency claims |
| Structure definition | explicit class/component kind, single base reference with legal kind direction, owned ports, contained instance features, private variables, owned executable declarations as applicable |
| Instance feature | explicit class/component usage kind, exactly one kind-compatible definition target, composite ownership, multiplicity one |
| Port feature | interface target, role, optionality and derived member directions |
| QoS policy | package-owned declaration identity, complete ordered body, source provenance; no runtime queues |
| Connection/delegation | owner, endpoint feature references, explicit path provenance, interface identity, queue policy for a connection; no queue for delegation |
| Variable/local/parameter | declared type, ownership/scope, initializer where present, mutable copied-value semantics |
| Executable | explicit operation/function kind, signature, trigger, concurrency/timing, locally owned calculations and one statement or state body, context bound to the owning class/component |
| Class/component root/binding | explicit root kind, named occurrence with inline composition or compatible class/component-definition reference, external destination identities, explicit/effective deployment and effective service/realtime/mixed profile |
| Requirement/case/view | immutable attributes and doc, satisfy/verify edges, case kind and step graph, view kind and ordered selected occurrence paths |

All textual references MUST resolve before normalization. A `TypeRef` is a use, not a new nominal declaration. Array extents are carried as shape constraints and do not create element aliasing. Empty records have one value. void results create no output-value feature. Parameters preserve order, independent of parameter names at a handler implementation.

A typed root expands its compatible class/component definition, and an inline root expands its authored children; no extra wrapper occurrence is inserted. Its normalized profile records service even when the source omits that default, while provenance preserves the omission. A typed binding body contributes only deployments, never topology. Every component child expands its definition's child features recursively. Expansion preserves each connector's owner path, so two uses of the same component produce distinct connector occurrences and queues. Effective deployment is computed by the nearest explicitly deployed ancestor, including the occurrence itself. An absent ancestor yields an explicit Unallocated value, not an invented external system.

Specialization preserves structural kinds: component may specialize class or component; class may specialize class only. Class usages may reference either definition kind; component usages require component definitions. Operation-to-function and class-usage-to-component-usage redefinition may narrow kinds while preserving every other compatibility obligation; reverse widening is invalid.

Specialization normalizes to an explicit base-definition reference and an ordered effective member set. Each inherited member retains declaration provenance; each replacement additionally references the inherited member it redefines. Expansion instantiates the effective set separately for every component instance, including root-owned functions and variables. Local references in inherited bodies resolve in that effective instance context. A mapper MUST preserve redefinition compatibility checks and MUST NOT merge stores merely because they share an inherited declaration.

External deployment destinations retain their exact source identity string and an optional host-provided resolved external identity. Normalization must distinguish unresolved external references from local path errors; it cannot fabricate an imported SysML part.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-73-contract-projection-and-route-normalization"></a>

## 7.3 Contract projection and route normalization

For every port occurrence and interface member, derive a member projection with contract/member identity and role-specific directions:

| Role | Data/event projection | RPC request | RPC result/failure |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| sender / receiver | out / in data | absent | absent |
| publisher / subscriber | out / in event | absent | absent |
| client / server | absent | out / in | in / out |
| requires / provides | in / out event | out / in | in / out |

Contract signatures remain shared; these projections belong to specific port occurrences. One bit on a service port cannot replace this table. After following delegation to its resolved locally handled port, every receiving projection references exactly one handling function in that component context. Each outgoing data/event projection references the nonempty set of syntactic emission sites in its resolved context. Those links do not assert reachability.

Resolve a connection by following each boundary port's unique delegation chain to a locally owned handling/emitting component port. Preserve the ordered original boundary chain as provenance. The normalized route is `(connectorOccurrence, sourceLeafPort, destinationLeafPort, interface)`. Delegation aliases do not add storage, queues, dispatch, retries or observable messages. Distinct connectors resolving to the same directed leaf pair are invalid. Optionality is checked at every port along a boundary chain; one optional declaration cannot erase a required declaration.

A connector policy normalizes to its complete effective body, whether selected inline or by a named `QosPolicy`. For a named selection, preserve the declaration reference and both declaration/use source locations; for an inline selection, preserve its body/use provenance. `Qos` is the authoring selection and `QosBody` is its field grouping, not a runtime object. Policies cannot contain policy references or overrides. Multiple uses of one declaration still produce independent connector occurrences.

A queue key is `(connectorOccurrence, direction, memberIdentity)` with direction relative to the connector's declared orientation. RPC responses and requests use opposite directions. Service events use the response direction but their distinct event member identities prevent queue sharing with results. Correlation state is separate from queue storage.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-74-executable-normalization"></a>

## 7.4 Executable normalization

Every operation/function has a behavior declaration and one synthetic executable feature owned by its component structure. The feature is typed by that behavior and binds an activation to a component occurrence. It MUST NOT create a user-visible performer, an independently editable implementation allocation, or permission to call it across a component boundary.

A statement body normalizes to an entry, ordered steps, exit/failure terminals and lexical scopes. Every step records its source construct and ordered operands. The following lowerings are required:

| Source construct | Normalized meaning |
| --- | --- |
| var / assignment | evaluate expression, then create/replace copied local or private value |
| send / publish | operand evaluation followed by per-route message offers, emission provenance and success/rejection continuation |
| routed call | issuance/correlation creation, suspended frame, timeout, typed success binding or terminating failure branch |
| local call | synchronous same-component call edge, copied arguments and results, fresh non-void result binding, borrowed activation context/snapshot and caller continuation |
| return / fail | terminate the current frame; unwind callers on failure; correlated result/failure for RPC activations |
| if | evaluate bool once, mutually exclusive selected branch and merge of control only |
| while | repeated condition, body, iteration counter and bound-failure edge; no implicit unbounded loop |
| for | loop-owned initializer scope, initial test, ordered body/update/test, counter and bound-failure edge; update is an assignment grouping |
| forEach | one copied fixed-array snapshot, finite element order, fresh typed element/body scope for each iteration |
| do | initial body execution followed by condition, counter and bound-failure edge; condition excludes body locals |
| interruptible | named armed region, typed input reference, normal/interrupt exits, handler payload scope and explicit stack/correlation cancellation |
| assert | bool evaluation and failure edge when false |
| calc | function-owned typed inputs, named output and expression tree with parameter-only environment and explicit acyclic calculation call edges |

Expressions carry inferred type and evaluation order. Binary short-circuit nodes conditionally skip their right operand. A record value maps fields to declared identities, not input order; an array preserves value order and fixed extent; enum values retain enum and literal identity. Numeric failures and invalid index edges are part of the expression contract. A mapper MUST NOT silently substitute mathematical unbounded integers for SWAML bounded int execution.

The directly owned state body is syntax grouping, not an independent behavior declaration. It contributes to its function's normalization: state identities, initial reference, entry/exit/effect step groups, trigger matchers, pure guards, priorities, finality, current-state feature, timer-generation features and the chapter 6 dispatch/reaction rules. A transition is not merely an unconstrained edge: its priority selection, source-state condition, payload environment and ordered exit/effect/update/entry phases are required semantics.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-75-kernel-representation-and-swaml-library-responsibilities"></a>

## 7.5 Kernel representation and SWAML library responsibilities

The `kernel` field of the [mapping inventory](../spec/kerml-mapping.json) lists proposed KerML bases; the `semantic` field names the SWAML meaning to preserve. Empty kernel lists mean a lexical/grouping node contributes to its owner's representation, not that its meaning may be discarded. The inventory covers every authored grammar rule and type alias; punctuation and reserved keywords are covered by their productions.

Kernel ownership, membership, typing, parameter, feature-value and reference relationships MUST be instantiated according to the selected KerML backend. The inventory is not a recipe to instantiate abstract metaclasses blindly. A backend must choose suitable concrete subclasses for abstract bases such as Expression and validate all resulting relationships. Source grouping rules do not generate duplicate nodes for the same AST element.

The SWAML library supplies typed semantics for activation/context, copied values, versioned port contracts, queue offers, request correlation, failure outcomes, bounded flow and machine reactions. A communication lowering uses a Step typed by the library's send/publish/request behavior, with typed features for its ports, member and payload. It MUST NOT serialize a made-up kernel class called `SWAML-library-message-interaction`. The kernel Step is the base; the library behavior is the additional meaning.

Requirements, timing, QoS, deployment, case and view annotations use project-owned metadata definitions with typed fields and explicit references. Cases additionally carry a scenario behavior/step graph and happens-before edges; they do not become an independently executing program. A view's selections use semantic identities and occurrence contexts; geometry/layout stays in a separate sidecar.

A K-conforming artifact MUST include or resolvably identify its exact SWAML library version and declare which constraints are checked. Carrying uninterpreted metadata is preservation, not proof that a generic kernel tool understands or executes it. The project has not yet selected/published a library namespace or implemented this backend; these obligations define its acceptance criteria.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-76-case-and-view-normalization"></a>

## 7.6 Case and view normalization

Normalize each case label to a distinct step identity, resolve its route/member and orientation, and translate `after` to an explicit happens-before edge. The transitive closure determines causal ancestry. A reply links to its unique unanswered request ancestor with reversed endpoints and the same connector/member. A request is already answered for candidate selection only if its reply is also a causal ancestor; an independent branch cannot settle the choice. Ambiguous candidates and multiple replies to one request are invalid. Source position contributes display order only. An unanswered request is allowed in a partial trace; no synthetic reply is inserted.

A case realization claim records a witness execution and assumptions, including payload values absent from the diagram, dispatch choices and function paths. Mere route validity does not supply that witness. A graphical renderer can display an unverified case but must not show it as proven.

Normalize views to `(kind, selectedOccurrences[])`, retaining the authored selection/filter clauses for round trips. Compact of syntax contributes one selection. Resolve include subjects in the owning package, expand recursive descendants, then evaluate typed Boolean predicates and deduplicate occurrence paths in first-selection order. gv/grv may select any named semantic element or package except a view; iv selects classes/components; cv/sv select cases or roots; fv/tv select executable operations/functions. Compact tv subjects are operations/functions; selecting descendants requires an explicit recursive include and a filter that leaves only executable subjects. A kind predicate matches the exact authored kind: class differs from component and operation differs from function. Use explicit or predicates to include both; signature remains a contract-member kind, not executable behavior. Paths traverse effective component members, owned named behavior elements and interface members exposed through ports. A package/declaration name collision is ambiguous.

A trace-backed tv presentation additionally references an identified run/trace and its selected function occurrences. A view without trace evidence may show declared contracts but MUST NOT invent observed phases. Preserve activation identities, event ordinals, correlations and observed phase boundaries separately from declared timing contracts. Horizon/stopped intervals remain open; zero-cost execution cannot be normalized as measured physical CPU time. Timing geometry and interactive filters remain presentation data. The tv kind is retained as presentation metadata; it introduces no activation, execution step or scheduling participant.

GV Box in box and Tree layouts normalize to the same ordered selected occurrences and semantic graph. The layout choice, geometry, bends and collapsed regions belong to a presentation sidecar. A layout round trip MUST preserve definition identity, occurrence paths, ownership and all typed references/routes. An ownership tree edge cannot replace a connector or turn a reference into containment.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-77-equivalence-and-completeness"></a>

## 7.7 Equivalence and completeness

Two normalized artifacts are equivalent when an identity-respecting graph correspondence preserves ownership, types, value shapes, interface versions, instance contexts, routes, evaluation order, triggers, state selection, policies, deployments, case dependencies/correlations and ordered view selections. Requirement attributes, formal docs and satisfy/verify identities must be preserved. Whitespace, ordinary comments, source offsets and diagram geometry are excluded from execution equivalence but remain required authoring data. Failure reasons remain observable diagnostic values.

A round trip may rename anonymous lowering IDs while retaining their owner/role trace. It may not remove guarded branches, reorder effects, merge queues, weaken timing contracts, convert copied payloads into remote references, or drop SWAML metadata because a receiver lacks support. Unsupported meaning requires an explicit partial-import result. This information-preservation contract is complete for the closed 1.3 syntax; implementing and validating a KerML backend is a separate milestone.

<a id="source-0db79669e756-requirement-and-presentation-extension"></a>

## Requirement and presentation extension

The rules in [chapter 4](#source-1f8b7bb89e5f) preserve requirements as named obligations with immutable attributes and at most one owned formal doc. Satisfaction references a design identity and requirement identity; verification references a verificationCase identity and requirement identity. Use cases cannot own verification links. Ordinary comments retain source extents and owner identities separately from formal documentation. Cases and links express intent, with no automatic proof status. View include/filter clauses resolve to a stable union of occurrence paths and have no execution steps or store effects. These constructs remain part of the proposed mapping contract; no kernel exporter is implemented.

---

<a id="source-34105487b7a4"></a>

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-6-operational-contract-and-target-bindings"></a>

# 6. Operational contract and target bindings

Normative chapter of the [SWAML 1.3 specification](#source-ffe5ff67da37). This chapter makes the allowed traces precise; it does not prescribe an implementation thread model. The [reference simulation binding](#source-fedc6631cb48) makes a deterministic logical-time choice within this contract; production backends require their own binding and evidence.

Class and component usages share this operational contract; operation and function activations likewise share it. The kind discriminators remain part of source and normalized identities, but neither class spelling nor operation spelling adds heap references, unrestricted object dispatch, a new scheduler or a new runtime profile. Component is a specialized Class, and Function a specialized Operation. The structural compatibility and handler-kind restrictions in chapters 1â€“3 are checked before execution.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-61-configuration-and-observable-occurrences"></a>

## 6.1 Configuration and observable occurrences

A running configuration consists of: the selected root component instance tree; private variable stores per component instance; live activation frames and their local call stacks; one serial lock per component; the state and timer generation of each live stateful function; message queues keyed by connector occurrence, direction and member; pending request correlations; released lifecycle activations; and a monotonic logical time.

Each message has a logical identity, original emission time, producing activation identity, typed copied payload, source/destination port occurrences, interface member, connector occurrence and delegation route. A retry keeps the logical identity and original timestamp. A transport attempt has a separate identity. An RPC response additionally references its request correlation. Occurrence identities MUST be unique within a run and cannot be declaration IDs reused for every emission.

The minimum observable trace alphabet is initialization, release, dispatch, statement completion, emission, admission, drop, delivery, request completion, timeout, state entry/exit, activation completion/failure, and contract violation. Each record includes logical time, occurrence identity and relevant source identities. A backend may omit detailed statement traces during normal execution, but MUST provide sufficient checking instrumentation to diagnose violated obligations. Trace records and diagnostics are observations, not implicit interface messages.

A step of execution is either a local statement/expression step, communication admission/delivery, dispatch, reaction microstep, or time advancement. Independent components may step in either order. No text-file order between components creates a happens-before edge. Elapsed physical execution time must be represented when checking deadlines; treating each statement as zero-time cannot establish production timing compliance.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-62-initialization-and-release"></a>

## 6.2 Initialization and release

Initialization has a barrier with the following phases:

1. Expand the acyclic instance tree, establish effective deployments, create private stores from pure initializers, resolve all routes and install all queues/handlers.
2. Install each stateful function's initial state identity and arm its initialization reaction. It is not yet ready to consume a stimulus. Register all ordinary startup activations.
3. Establish one run epoch `t0`. Release every startup/initialization activation at `t0`; activate periodic releases at `t0 + k * period`, with k starting at 1. All ports may now admit traffic.

The scheduler may interleave startup activations across components and within a component subject to its lock. Messages arriving before a machine finishes initial entry remain in their connector queue; they are not discarded merely because initial entry is pending. Timers start when entry finishes, not when the initialization reaction was released. A startup function may request a service whose component's startup activation has not run: handlers and initialized data already exist. If an application needs an additional readiness protocol, it MUST model that protocol through an interface.

Each startup activation is released exactly once per root run. The core defines no automatic component restart. A deployment implementation restarting a failed component starts a new run epoch for the affected binding and MUST report its reset, queue-retention and correlation policy; it cannot imply uninterrupted E conformance for a reset it has not modeled.

Periodic releases are independent of earlier completions. Serial dispatch does not merge or silently skip them. Input-triggered work remains in the input queue until dispatch is possible; it is not copied into an unbounded second activation queue. Admission of an RPC response resumes its suspended frame without acquiring another component lock.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-63-dispatch-backlog-and-snapshots"></a>

## 6.3 Dispatch, backlog and snapshots

A serial activation root acquires its component's lock at dispatch and releases it on completion/failure. Local calls execute on the activation root's stack and inherit its concurrency mode and snapshot, regardless of a helper's own timing declaration. A reentrant activation root may overlap other activation roots, but neither it nor any reachable helper may assign component data. Assigning its own parameters/locals is allowed.

A reentrant activation root captures an atomic value snapshot of all component variables at dispatch, before its first expression. All reads by that activation and its helpers use that snapshot throughout suspension and resumption. An assignment by a serial activation is atomic at the whole-value level; snapshots observe some consistent store between assignments. A machine's next reaction captures a fresh snapshot if it is reentrant. Reentrant does not allow overlapping reactions of the same machine.

Between independent ready queues and lifecycle releases, the scheduler chooses any enabled activation root. For a FIFO queue, dispatch preserves queue order; completion order of reentrant handlers need not match dispatch order. A target backend MUST specify fairness and scheduling policy, and MUST NOT introduce starvation while claiming eventual at-least-once delivery under continuously enabled receivers.

An executable target binding MUST bound pending start/periodic/timer activations separately from connector queues. On exceeding that bound it MUST record `ActivationOverflow` and stop the affected root execution; silently dropping or coalescing releases is not an implementation of this core. A simulator may expose an explicitly unbounded mathematical backlog, but cannot use it as evidence for a bounded production deployment. The binding also bounds simultaneous reentrant activation roots; when full, subsequent work remains pending rather than exceeding the limit.

Operator and test inputs follow ordinary typed routes from their emitting functions. A reference host MUST NOT introduce an unmodeled activation or message-injection channel. A generated test driver is part of the validated source and run evidence; its event emissions participate in the same queue admission, delivery, transport timing and handler ownership rules as application emissions.

WCET includes local helpers and active reaction microsteps; request wait consumes deadline budget but no active CPU budget. The activation root's deadline is measured from release. For input reactions release occurs when the message is ready in its destination queue. A timer reaction is released at its due time even if dispatch is later. Local helpers have no independent release; a backend checks their declared execution bounds over the invocation and also includes them in the activation root's bounds.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-64-queue-offers-and-fanout"></a>

## 6.4 Queue offers and fanout

`send` and `publish` evaluate all arguments once and snapshot the matching active connector set. They offer a distinct per-connector logical message to **every** connector in that set, even if an earlier offer rejects. Offers are independent and have no specified interconnector order. The statement finishes after all local admission decisions. If any offer rejects, the activation fails with an admission failure; successful offers remain committed. Loss through dropOldest/dropNewest is not rejection and does not fail the sender. Atomic broadcast requires a modeled protocol.

For a given queue, offer and dispatch are atomic decisions. Capacity counts retained messages waiting for dispatch, including ones whose transport availability is pending; it excludes running handlers. Removing an old message or discarding a new one MUST be observable to a checking executor. An optional port with no connectors produces no offers and succeeds for data/events.

| Condition | Data/event emission | RPC request | RPC response |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| No live endpoint | optional unbound succeeds; failed bound route reports transport failure | `Unavailable` if unbound; otherwise `Transport` when detected | requester gets `Transport` if detectable, otherwise timeout |
| Full queue with reject | producer activation fails after all fanout offers | caller executes `otherwise` with `Rejected` | requester gets `Rejected` if the binding can report it; otherwise timeout; server's completed effects remain |
| dropOldest/dropNewest | dropped occurrence is observable; statement can succeed | dropped request waits until timeout unless transport reports failure | dropped response leaves request waiting until timeout |
| Age expired | discard before dispatch | no service dispatch; caller eventually times out unless already failed | discard; no successful completion |
| Receiver handler fails | receiver records local failure; sender is not retrospectively failed | correlated `RemoteFailure` | not a second handler; resume request failure path |

Control failures such as admission rejection use the same request route/correlation. They are transport outcomes of that interface exchange, not arbitrary model event channels. A binding unable to signal a failure cannot fabricate a successful result.

An admitted message becomes ready no later than emission plus maxLatency when such a bound is claimed. Deadline miss is a violation even if the message is later delivered; it is not itself a new delivery policy. `maxAge` eligibility is inclusive: age <= maxAge may dispatch; age > maxAge is expired. Discarded/expired messages have no eventual-delivery obligation. Without maxAge, asynchronous messages have no core expiry before connection teardown; a backend must maintain its delivery obligation or explicitly report loss of availability. Retry policy, finite memory and resource exhaustion behavior belong in the target binding, not undocumented defaults.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-65-rpc-completion-and-races"></a>

## 6.5 RPC completion and races

A request correlation has states Pending, Succeeded or Failed. Creation precedes the first request offer. Its expiry is issuance time plus timeout. At most one terminal completion may win. A response can succeed only if it is observed at the requester at a time **strictly earlier** than expiry and the correlation is Pending. At expiry timeout wins ties. Terminal state is immutable; later responses and duplicate replies are discarded and observable.

AtLeastOnce may dispatch multiple handler activations with the same logical request/correlation. Idempotency must cover storage writes and published side effects; returning the same value alone is insufficient. The first eligible response completes the caller. Retrying must never create a new caller-visible correlation or extend its timeout. A binding may deduplicate repeated requests while preserving required behavior.

The mandatory `as name` on a void RPC labels successful completion without introducing an inhabitable value. It reserves that name in the enclosing lexical scope but it MUST NOT be read or assigned as data. A void handler uses bare `return;`. The failure block has an implicit copied `failure: string`; an existing live value with that name is a static error. Failure blocks cannot fall through to the successful continuation.

A timeout does not retract messages already sent or cancel a server frame. Pending requests cannot be used as detached futures; each statement awaits its own outcome. Synchronous dependency cycles remain expressible but may time out. A static validator is not required to prove their absence; an execution/timing claim must account for them.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-interruption-of-pending-continuations"></a>

### Interruption of pending continuations

An accepted region interrupt resumes its existing activation at a statement boundary, retaining the serial lock and activation deadline. Its input is admitted, delayed, ordered, expired or dropped through the same per-route queue rules as other data/events. It is delivered only while its selected region is armed; available input outside that interval is discarded with an undeliverable-interrupt observation. It does not release an independent activation or create an unbounded handler queue.

Cancellation unwinds the protected stack to that region, disarms it, and enters its handler with copied payload bindings and the enclosing lexical environment. If a caller-side request is Pending, cancellation makes it terminal Failed with an Interrupted reason and records requestCancelled. Its success and otherwise continuations are both abandoned. Scheduled resume work for the unwound stack is invalidated. Server execution and committed effects remain unchanged. A later reply observes the terminal correlation and is discarded.

A response or timeout already terminal keeps its original outcome. In particular, expiry still wins the response/timeout tie. A subsequent interrupt may transfer control before the already queued terminal continuation runs; this does not change the recorded correlation outcome. A normally completed interrupt handler continues after the region. An outer interrupt can unwind an inner region or its handler, including a second suspended request. Return/failure still disarms all regions unwound with the frame. The original activation deadline applies through cancellation and handler completion.

A conforming trace identifies region entry/exit, interrupt delivery and cancelled correlations with their source region, activation, member and route identities. Reference simulation uses one-shot region watches and continuation generations to prevent stale scheduling from resuming cancelled frames. Physical cancellation latency remains a target-binding obligation.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-66-state-reaction-ordering"></a>

## 6.6 State reaction ordering

A machine has one initialization reaction and then a sequence of reactions. Each reaction includes all immediately enabled completion transitions until quiescent/final/failure. Completion selection precedes dispatch of the next external stimulus. If no completion guard holds, the machine becomes quiescent. A write by another function does not itself awaken completion transitions; they are reconsidered after the next machine reaction, including one that consumed an unhandled message or expired a timer with no enabled transition.

On completing a state's entry, create one timer stimulus per distinct `after` duration on its outgoing transitions, tagged with the current state-entry generation. All transitions with that duration compete using guard/priority. Distinct durations produce distinct stimuli. Leaving a state cancels every timer from that generation. A queued timer from an obsolete generation is discarded. Reentering, including a self-transition, creates a new generation. Entry code time is outside the residence timer; the timer measures time since entry completion.

A due timer and a ready input are independent stimuli. Their selection order is unspecified unless the target scheduler constrains it. Transition priority selects among transitions for the **same** stimulus; it is not a task/queue priority. A consumed stimulus is not retained merely because all guards were false. Entry/exit blocks have no event payload scope. Exit and effect run before state identity changes; destination entry follows that change.

The completion microstep counter is zero at the start of a reaction and increases once per taken completion transition. Taking a 1,025th completion transition fails the reaction before executing that transition. External dispatch does not occur between those microsteps. Guards are pure expressions and cannot send, mutate, or consume another message. A guard evaluation failure fails the owning activation.

Final entry completion, bare return or failure terminates the machine, cancels all its timers and disables its dispatch. Future messages to its members become undeliverable occurrences; they are not rerouted to another function. Other functions in the same component may remain alive. No implicit exit behavior runs on return/failure unless it has already been reached in the specified transition phases.

<a id="source-34105487b7a4-67-execution-binding-contract"></a>

## 6.7 Execution binding contract

Every backend claiming E MUST identify its backend version, supported SWAML version, selected root component, model digest and target configuration. It MUST supply explicit values or a justified symbolic abstraction for:

- effective deployment of every component; clock basis, precision and cross-target clock-error bounds;
- scheduler, fairness, serial locking, snapshot implementation and reentrant limits;
- lifecycle backlog limits and overflow observation;
- transport, serialization, copying/isolation, queue storage and retry/deduplication policy;
- deadline/latency/age measurement points and what happens after a reported violation;
- external adapter behavior and assumptions, startup readiness, shutdown/reset and queue retention;
- resource failure handling, correlation uniqueness, observability and evidence artifacts.

Timing/resource failure policies may stop execution after recording a violation. Continuing is permitted only with the violation visible, and cannot count as a satisfying execution for the violated contract. An execution trace is conforming when all observed steps obey core transitions; satisfying a particular model additionally requires its declared assertions and contracts to hold. A runtime that correctly reports a failed assertion is not thereby a broken interpreter.

Security and target-specific bus/OS policies can strengthen this contract, but cannot add hidden model exchanges. AUTOSAR Classic task/RTE mappings, Adaptive service bindings and broker configurations must preserve the same identities, member directions and copied-value boundaries.

For each mapped function the binding MUST identify its activation source and preserve component-wide serial exclusion or the declared reentrant snapshot semantics. For each mapped interface member it MUST identify the data conversion and transport route, including response correlation where applicable. A platform read/write API, shared-memory optimization or callback must preserve the observable copied-value and queue semantics of the modeled exchange.

For AUTOSAR Classic, the binding MUST state how modeled activations map to runnable events and tasks, and how sampled or queued members map to RTE communication. For Adaptive, it MUST state how the fixed modeled endpoints map to service instances and how discovery, unavailability and application startup affect pending exchanges. For web and cloud targets, it MUST state how requests, notifications, broker acknowledgments, durable state and restarts relate to modeled completion and failure. A transport acknowledgment is not automatically a successful application response, and a connector queue is not automatically durable storage.

Primitive values retain the domains in chapter 1. A binding to fixed-width platform integers, bounded strings, schema fields or wire encodings MUST define conversion limits and observable failure for unsupported values, or provide evidence that the model stays within the supported subset. It MUST NOT silently truncate, wrap, reinterpret or share mutable payloads. Target-specific restrictions and evidence belong with the mapping; accepting a core model does not certify a platform implementation.

---

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48"></a>

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-reference-simulation-profile-13"></a>

# Reference simulation profile 1.3

This document specifies the **swaml-reference/1.3** execution binding. This profile executes unified components, inherited effective members, executable functions and pure calculations; each connector retains its independent effective policy and queues. The portable TypeScript [interpreter](../src/simulation/simulator.ts) and [value evaluator](../src/simulation/values.ts) execute valid SWAML 1.3 root components. The profile implements logical behavior with explicit resource bounds. It does not model instruction duration, operating-system scheduling, hardware or network deployment.

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-run-a-model"></a>

## Run a model

Build with `npm ci` and `npm run build`. Then run:

```sh
npm run simulate -- Commerce::Shop examples/simulation/03-service-pubsub.json examples/03-service-pubsub.swaml
npm run simulate -- Lifecycle::Product examples/simulation/04-stateful-function.json examples/04-stateful-function.swaml
node scripts/check-simulations.mjs
```

The command takes a qualified root component name, a JSON options file and all workspace model files. It validates the entire input before executing. JSON output contains the trace, final stores, machine states, effective deployments, exact source text and per-source SHA-256 digests. Redirect stdout to a file when collecting evidence. The direct `node out/src/cli/simulate.js ...` command emits only JSON to stdout on a valid invocation; `npm run` additionally prints its script banner.

The selected root may compose components inline or instantiate a component definition. A typed root's deployment body does not extend its topology. Instance paths, stores and deployment keys remain relative to the selected root without a root-name prefix. Root-owned functions use their local name, such as `initialize`, without a root prefix. Store/deployment output includes every expanded component, with the root represented by the empty-string path. Source deployment may explicitly name the root to establish its default location. The output names the selected architecture `root`, and identifies the binding as `swaml-reference/1.3`.

Exit status is 0 when no failure was observed, 1 for invalid models, a failed activation, a contract violation or a resource limit, and 2 for an invocation/configuration error. A bounded horizon with no observed failure is not proof of eventual success. Descriptive `case` declarations are not a second executable script and are not automatically proven by this command.

The [run catalog](../examples/simulation/runs.json) names the published roots, options and expected final values. The [hello trace](../examples/simulation/hello-trace.json) is a complete reproducible observation artifact, including its exact model source. CI reruns every catalog entry, compares final values, checks repeatability and detects a stale published trace.

Operator and test stimuli are named interface events published by ordinary functions in the model. Start behavior and finite state/timer sequences schedule those emissions. Their explicit routes use the same queues, QoS and latency as application messages. The simulator has no direct function-release or destination-event injection option; a generated driver must be validated and preserved as source. See [events and external inputs](#source-a75326096d8c).

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-binding-parameters"></a>

## Binding parameters

All times and counts are safe integers. Times are nanoseconds. Unknown options and invalid numeric bounds are rejected.

| JSON property | Default / domain | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `untilNs` | required; â‰¥ 0 | Process events at times â‰¤ this horizon. Start epoch is zero. |
| `maxSteps` | 100000; â‰¥ 1 | Fuel for expansion, events, statements, expressions and transition work. Stops explicitly on exhaustion; it is a resource count, not elapsed CPU time. |
| `maxPending` | 1024; â‰¥ 1 | Global bound on pending lifecycle/periodic/timer work, excluding active frames and connector queues. |
| `maxReentrant` | 64; â‰¥ 1 | Maximum simultaneous reentrant activation roots per component. Serial activation roots have their separate one-lock rule. |
| `latencyNs` | 0; â‰¥ 0 | Constant one-way transport delay for accepted request, response, data and event offers. |
| `faults` | empty list | Fault schedule keyed by the one-based `offer` ordinal shown in the trace. Each offer has at most one entry. |

Example options:

```json
{
  "untilNs": 3000000000,
  "latencyNs": 1000,
  "maxSteps": 100000,
  "maxPending": 1024,
  "maxReentrant": 64,
  "faults": [{ "offer": 1, "kind": "drop" }]
}
```

Driver publications follow ordinary source statement order and scheduler rules. Named events carry copied, typed payloads through their declared routes. Independent source activations retain the deterministic scheduling order described below; the simulator has no separate input-release list.

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-deterministic-scheduling-and-time"></a>

## Deterministic scheduling and time

The event ordering key is `(timeNs, phase, insertionOrdinal)`. At one timestamp, phase 0 processes request expiries; phase 1 processes lifecycle/timer releases and transport readiness; phase 2 advances execution; phase 3 checks deadlines/latency. Thus timeout wins an exactly simultaneous response, while an activation completing exactly at its deadline satisfies the bound. Each event is followed by a dispatch pass: pending lifecycle work in release order, then connector queues in first-offer order. Enabled work may acquire a lock during that pass; newly released work does not preempt an already held serial lock.

Execution advances between completed statements, local function steps, loop iterations and state transitions. Pure expressions and individual assignments are atomic. Root activations interleave through the stable event ordinal. FIFO delivery is maintained per connector/direction/member. `unordered` permits this FIFO choice too; the simulator does not randomize it. Independent queues have a deterministic tie-break for reproducibility, not a source-language happens-before relation.

All local CPU costs are abstracted to zero. Transport delay, timeout, period, timer residence, suspension and queue delay advance logical time. Deadline observation begins at lifecycle/timer release or input readiness, including a message awaiting a busy component. Helpers receive their own elapsed deadline watchdog at invocation. A detected deadline or latency violation stops this run and remains in the trace. Zero-cost execution cannot establish a physical WCET bound; external deployment destinations retain their identities and do not become simulated processors.

Serial activation roots retain their lock while awaiting RPC. Reentrant activation roots read a deep dispatch-time snapshot; their helpers share that snapshot and mode. Reentrant activation roots may coexist with the serial activation root, subject to the reentrant limit. A machine allows one reaction at a time. Input stays in its connector queue until the relevant dispatch limit/lock permits execution. Pending lifecycle capacity is checked before adding another release; overflow stops with `ActivationOverflow`.

The finite-fuel policy also bounds non-progressing or Zeno model executions. No fairness or liveness result beyond the observed finite trace is claimed. There is no randomness, wall-clock dependency or automatic restart.

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-transport-and-fault-interpretation"></a>

## Transport and fault interpretation

Each connector occurrence, direction and member owns a separate queue. Delegation resolves boundary aliases to locally handled component ports without adding queues. Capacity includes messages waiting for transport readiness; dispatched handlers are excluded. A send/publish attempts every matching route before reporting rejection. Offers that succeeded remain committed.

| Fault `kind` | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `drop` | For bestEffort/atMostOnce, discard the admitted message observably. For atLeastOnce, lose the first attempt, retain the logical identity/timestamp and retry once after an additional `max(1, delayNs)`; the retry succeeds unless overflow, age or availability terminates its obligation. |
| `duplicate` | Only legal on atLeastOnce. Dispatch two attempts of the same logical message/correlation. The retained logical queue entry reserves capacity until both attempts have been dispatched, with both preceding the next FIFO message. Response duplicates are observed and ignored after terminal completion. |
| `transport` | Detect a failed route immediately at emission. Report `Transport` to the relevant producer/correlation. This is an interface transport outcome, not a free event. |

An entry may set `delayNs` to override the normal delay for that offer. Default transport is reliable with one attempt for every admitted offer; this is a permitted trace of each delivery policy. The finite injected loss/retry model exercises failure rules but does not simulate arbitrary networks or prove eventual delivery. An entry targeting an offer beyond the chosen horizon remains unused; inspect `emission.offer` to verify that the intended fault was exercised.

Queue reject fails a data/event producer after fanout. RPC admission rejection enters `otherwise` with `Rejected`. Drop policies do not fail the producer. Age is measured from original emission, inclusive at the declared maximum, with expiry on the next nanosecond. A timed-out request does not cancel server effects. Remote handler failure returns `RemoteFailure`; late/duplicate replies cannot resume a caller again. An optional unbound RPC reports `Unavailable`; an unbound optional data/event send succeeds without offers.

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-values-flow-and-state-coverage"></a>

## Values, flow and state coverage

Every expression and statement in the grammar is interpreted: primitives, enums, nominal records, fixed arrays, field/index reads, calculations, local functions, copied parameters, lexical scopes, assignment, branches, bounded loops, assertions, returns, explicit failure, send, publish and awaited request. int arithmetic uses exact intermediate integer operations before range checks. real uses binary64. Scalar byte/uint/int/real widening preserves values. Checked assignment into uint or byte rejects values outside the destination domain. Payloads, parameters and results are copied values.

State initialization, entry/exit, event payloads, guards, priority, timer generations, self transitions, completion reactions and finality are interpreted. Entry/exit lack event payload scope. Stale timers and inputs to a terminated machine are observable discards. A return or failure terminates the machine without completing later transition phases. A guarded completion cycle fails before taking edge 1,025.

These rules have regression tests for both success and failure, including callback blocking, snapshots across suspension, fanout partial rejection, freshness boundaries, retry identity, duplicate RPC, exact-time timeout races, arithmetic/bounds failures, timer cancellation, backlog limits and queued-input deadlines. They exercise observable semantics rather than only mirroring parser implementation.

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-evidence-and-limitations"></a>

## Evidence and limitations

`status` reports why scheduling stopped: `quiescent` (no scheduled events), `horizon` (future events remain), `violation` (root contract/resource failure), or `limit` (fuel exhausted). `outcome` is `failed` when a failure/violation occurred and `no-failure-observed` otherwise. A failed ordinary activation need not stop unrelated components, so a quiescent run may still have a failed outcome. `timeNs` is the last processed event time, including inert expiry/deadline bookkeeping; it is not necessarily the horizon or last visible effect.

Trace records have monotonically increasing `ordinal`, nondecreasing `timeNs`, `kind`, and `subject`. Activation (`a`), correlation (`r`) and logical message (`m`) IDs are unique within a run; pair them with the evidence artifact's run identity when combining runs. Emissions carry producing activation, leaf endpoints, member, route, offer number and copied payload; source text preserves the boundary delegation provenance. Deliveries carry attempts and correlation where relevant. Statement records include source lines. Final stores and machine states remain available after limits and violations.

The CLI supplies SHA-256 per source; the portable library returns exact sources and leaves hashing/storage to its host. Keep those sources, digests, selected root, complete options, profile version and output together. Absolute source URIs may identify the local workspace; the published hello artifact uses stable repository-relative file URIs.

The implementation provides **reference simulation evidence with an explicit logical-time abstraction**. It is not a blanket E certification, model checker, production runtime, KerML exporter, AUTOSAR generator or graphical editor. Physical WCET/schedulability, distributed clock error, real serialization, middleware outages and independent conformance assessment still require separate evidence. A host can call the portable API after building and validating the entire referenced workspace.

<a id="source-fedc6631cb48-extended-control-flow-and-interruption"></a>

## Extended control flow and interruption

The 1.3 profile executes while, counted for, copied-array forEach and post-test do loops with their documented scopes and bounds. An armed interruptible region receives typed data/events through its connector queue, transfers control in its current serial activation, cancels a pending caller continuation and preserves earlier effects. Available input outside the armed interval is discarded explicitly. Region and cancellation events retain activation/correlation identities. See the [control-flow guide](#source-26fc5c2f4d44) for examples and the [operational contract](#source-34105487b7a4) for ties and late responses.

`projectFunctionTiming` derives observed ready/running/request-wait phases and markers from these traces. Declared timing values remain separate. Open horizon/stop intervals are retained, and zero-cost execution is never reported as physical CPU duration. Reproduce the four showcase traces with `node scripts/build-timing-demo.mjs`; the normal gate checks their exact generated projection.

---

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b"></a>

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b-1-structure-names-and-types"></a>

# 1. Structure, names and types

Normative chapter of the [SWAML 1.3 specification](#source-ffe5ff67da37).

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b-11-lexical-model"></a>

## 1.1 Lexical model

Source files MUST use UTF-8 and the `.swaml` extension. Whitespace and ordinary `//` line comments and `/* â€¦ */` block comments are insignificant to execution outside strings. `doc /* â€¦ */` is a formal owned specification, retained in the semantic model. Block comments do not nest. Identifiers match `[_a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*` and are case sensitive. Every quoted word in the grammar and the lexical doc marker are reserved. All keywords use lower camel case, including primitive types and view kinds. This version has no escaped identifiers.

Strings use double quotes and backslash escapes. A conforming implementation MUST support `\"`, `\\`, `\n`, `\r`, and `\t`; other escape sequences are invalid in conforming source. Raw control characters U+0000â€“U+001F, including line breaks and tabs, MUST NOT occur inside strings; use the permitted escapes. The lexer enforces this portable subset.

Unsigned decimal integers and decimal real literals are supported; a minus sign is a unary operator. Decimal real literals require digits on both sides of the point. Hexadecimal, exponent and suffix forms are absent. Duration literals consist of a positive integer followed by `ns`, `us`, `ms` or `s`. A duration MUST normalize to an exact integer nanosecond count no larger than 2^53âˆ’1. Time has no implicit unit.

The document contains zero or more packages, with no language header. The parser and its host MUST select SWAML 1.3 explicitly before loading the workspace; the CLI in this edition supports exactly 1.3. A host MUST NOT silently select whichever language version is newest. Historical `swaml "1.0";` headers are rejected by this grammar. An empty model is valid as an editing/library artifact, but contains no root component to execute.

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b-12-namespaces-and-resolution"></a>

## 1.2 Namespaces and resolution

Packages are top-level and non-nested in 1.3. A package name MUST be unique in the loaded model workspace. A package is declared in one file, although one file may contain several packages. Named elements in the same namespace MUST be unique; overload resolution is not supported.

`A::Type` identifies a package declaration. `import A::*;` exposes A's top-level declarations by short name in the importing package. Imports are not transitive. A short reference MUST resolve to exactly one declaration across the local package and its direct imports. Ambiguity is an error even when one candidate is local. Fully qualified references do not require an import.

Periods express containment/feature paths, not package qualification. A structural instance path follows actual child instances. A behavior communication path has exactly two identifiers: `localPort.member`. Directly following another component's data or function is forbidden. Pure record field paths follow only values already in the function's local environment.

Local function references resolve only in the owning component. Calculation references resolve only among calculations declared by the enclosing function, including its state guards and action blocks. State names resolve only inside the owning function's state body. Deployment destinations are external system identities; they do not participate in source-name lookup. No lookup fallback may expose a similarly named declaration from another component.

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b-13-definitions-usages-and-specialization"></a>

## 1.3 Definitions, usages and specialization

`class def C` declares a reusable software class; `component def C` declares the specialized component kind. In the software metamodel, **Component specializes Class**. Both kinds own child class/component usages, typed ports, connectors, delegation, private variables and executable operations/functions. Both leaf and composite types may own behavior. Class is not a value datatype or a shared heap object: every usage has independent storage and retains the same port boundary rules.

`class item: C;` declares one usage of a class or component definition. `component item: C;` requires a component definition. Multiplicity is exactly one; repeated usages require separate declarations. The declared usage kind and its referenced definition kind are both preserved. A class usage typed by a component is legal because every component is a class; the reverse is invalid.

A package may declare a **class or component execution root**: `class Demo { â€¦ }` and `component Demo { â€¦ }` supply inline composition and local behavior; `class Demo: Assembly;` and `component Demo: Assembly;` instantiate a compatible definition. A typed root may have a deployment-only body, for example `component Demo: Assembly realtime { deploy controller on "sysml:Vehicle::ecu"; }`. Its architecture and behavior come entirely from its definition; the binding body MUST NOT extend or redefine them. Inline roots may own the same structural and behavioral members as a definition, plus deployment bindings. All roots are closed execution contexts: every required port, including a root-owned port, is checked for closure.

A root has optional `service`, `realtime` or `mixed` profile following its name or type. Omission means `service`. A root is a named usage, never a definition, and cannot be used as a child's type. Definitions create no runtime participants until instantiated. The term root component in execution contracts includes either authored root kind unless a rule explicitly distinguishes them.

`class def Specialized specializes Base { â€¦ }` permits one class base. `component def Specialized specializes Base { â€¦ }` permits one class or component base. A class definition MUST NOT specialize a component definition, because that would erase the more specific component kind. The specialization graph MUST be acyclic. This is single inheritance of user definitions; the Component-to-Class metamodel relation is implicit and requires no built-in source declaration named Class.

Effective members consist of inherited members plus newly declared members, with explicit compatible replacements. Reusing an inherited name without `redefines` is an error; `redefines` without a matching inherited member is an error. For example, `redefines function initialize() on start { â€¦ }` may replace an inherited operation or function with the compatible specialized function kind. Redefinition replaces the whole member rather than merging bodies or private values. Inherited behavior resolves local member names in the effective specialized class/component.

Redefinition MUST retain the member family and exposed contract, with only kind-preserving or subtype-narrowing replacement. An operation may be replaced by a function; an inherited function MUST NOT become an operation. A class usage may be narrowed to a component usage with a compatible component type; an inherited component usage MUST NOT become a class usage. A port keeps its interface identity, role and optionality; a variable keeps its exact value type; a child usage keeps its declared definition type or specializes that type. An operation/function keeps its parameter names/types, result, statement/state body category, trigger kind/endpoint/period and concurrency contract, and does not increase a declared WCET or deadline. Connectors and delegations keep their endpoint paths. A replacement may change an initializer, compatible executable body (including its local calculations) or connector policy, subject to every ordinary validity rule. This reuse does not introduce dynamic creation, reference-valued objects, overloaded dispatch or unrestricted UML class semantics.

The effective containment graph MUST be acyclic, including inherited children. Every usage receives distinct private storage, local locks, activations and child usages. Two usages of the same definition require typed ports and explicit routes to communicate; sharing a definition never shares private state. Typed usage declarations cannot override private initializers; differing initial values require compatible generated specializations or an explicit modeled initialization protocol.

Library definitions may leave required ports unconnected until instantiated. A closed root MUST connect every nonoptional port after resolving delegation. A port can be handled by its owner's operations/functions or delegated to a child, but cannot simultaneously acquire two behavior owners. An optional port cannot weaken a required alias elsewhere on its route.

Class/component state is private to its usage. A variable has a declared type and a pure initializer independent of mutable variables and communication. Operation/function/calculation parameters and local variables MUST NOT shadow private variables or other live values in their scope. Blocks create lexical scopes. Parameters are copied input values; assignment changes the local copy only.

Throughout the remaining chapters, structural component ownership/execution rules apply to both class and component kinds, and executable function rules apply to both operation and function kinds, except where a signature or kind-compatibility rule distinguishes them. [ADR 0014](#source-e41a024287b1) records this hierarchy.

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b-14-value-types"></a>

## 1.4 Value types

| Type | Domain / semantics |
| --- | --- |
| `bool` | `true` or `false` |
| `int` | exact signed integers between âˆ’(2^53âˆ’1) and 2^53âˆ’1 |
| `uint` | exact integers from 0 through 2^53âˆ’1 |
| `byte` | exact integers from 0 through 255 |
| `real` | finite IEEE 754 binary64 values, with nearest-even arithmetic |
| `string` | finite Unicode text; no implicit encoding or transport size guarantee |
| `void` | no result value; the default when a function/signature omits `: Type`, never a variable, parameter, field, array or calculation result |
| `datatype` | a finite record with named typed fields |
| `enum` | a nominal set of one or more unique literals |
| `T[n]` | a fixed array of exactly n values, n a positive safe integer |

Datatypes and enums are nominal: two declarations with identical members are distinct types. Recursive records, including recursion through arrays, MUST be rejected. There are no pointers, references, nullable values, dynamic collections, aliases or generics in 1.3. `byte` is an unsigned eight-bit value domain. Other fixed-width encodings and bounded strings are target-binding choices; they are not inferred from these primitives.

`record T { field = expression; â€¦ }` MUST assign every field of T exactly once and no others. `T.Literal` MUST name a declared enum literal; a package-qualified form is `Package::T.Literal`. `[e1, e2, â€¦]` constructs a nonempty fixed array; its elements MUST have one nominal type or a numeric common type. Identical numeric element types are retained; mixed numeric types yield real if any is real, otherwise int if any is int, otherwise uint. A literal assigned to a declared array type checks each element against that element type, so `[0, 255]` can initialize `byte[2]`. Existing array values have invariant element type and extent. Arrays of differing lengths are incompatible.

Enum and local-value paths resolve deterministically. If the first segment of an unqualified dotted path is a live lexical/private value accessible in the current expression environment, the whole path is a local value read; a missing field is an error and MUST NOT fall back to an enum. Otherwise a two-segment `T.Literal` path resolves T among declarations in the current package and direct imports, checking ambiguity across all declaration kinds before requiring an enum. `Package::T.Literal` explicitly selects a loaded package enum without using the local-value environment. A wrong declaration kind, unknown literal, ambiguous name or array index applied to an enum is invalid. Calculations expose only parameters to that environment, and variable initializers expose no mutable values. A component variable therefore does not shadow an enum where that variable is inaccessible. No variant permits cross-component private-value lookup.

Record fields may be read by `value.field.subfield`. `arrayValue[index]` reads one array element, with an integral index. Only one final index suffix is available in 1.3; a local variable can hold an intermediate array value. Bounds are zero inclusive to length exclusive. Out-of-bounds access fails the current function at runtime and MUST NOT produce an undefined value. Whole-value assignment is supported; mutating a record field or array element in place is not part of 1.3.

Assignment, argument passing and return preserve nominal types and exact array lengths. Scalar numeric widening follows `byte â†’ uint â†’ int â†’ real`. Assigning an integral value to `uint` or `byte` performs a range check: a known out-of-range constant is a static error, and a computed out-of-range value fails execution. real values do not implicitly narrow to integral types. Arrays do not implicitly widen or narrow. Enum/integer conversion is absent.

Numeric overflow, non-finite results, division by zero and invalid indexing fail execution. The parser/validator rejects out-of-range literal integers, but proving arithmetic ranges requires execution or additional analysis.

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b-15-expression-rules"></a>

## 1.5 Expression rules

From lowest to highest precedence: `or`, `and`, `== !=`, `< <= > >=`, `+ -`, `* / %`, unary `not -`, primary expressions. Binary operators associate left. Parentheses override precedence. `and` and `or` short-circuit left-to-right. Other operands and call arguments are evaluated left-to-right.

Boolean operators require bool operands. Ordered comparison and arithmetic require numeric operands. Equality compares equal nominal value types recursively (records by declared fields, arrays by ordered values), or two numerics after promotion. string equality is exact code-point equality with no normalization; no ordering or concatenation operator is defined. `%` is integer remainder with the sign of the dividend and requires integral operands. `/` always returns real. Other arithmetic returns real if either input is real and int otherwise.

A calculation is declared only at the beginning of a function body, before its statement sequence or initial-state declaration. It is not a component member and cannot be independently inherited or redefined. Redefining the function replaces its whole calculation scope. A calculation has the syntax `calc twice { in x: real; return result: real = x * 2.0; }`. It declares zero or more ordered typed inputs and exactly one named non-void output defined by a pure expression. Its environment consists only of parameters, literals, record/enum/array construction and other calculations in the same function. It MUST NOT capture component variables, enclosing function parameters, block locals or state payloads, call functions, or communicate. Needed values must be passed as explicit arguments. Local call graphs MUST be acyclic in 1.3. `name(arguments)` evaluates the expression once with copied arguments and returns its value. The output name labels the result; it is not a mutable local or an input to its own expression. `twice(3.0)` supplies ordered copied inputs and yields the output value. Calculations can be used in local variable initializers, conditions, state guards/actions and other expressions of their enclosing function. Component variable initializers cannot call calculations. To reuse work across functions, define a local value-returning function and use a call statement. A calculation has no statement block, trigger, state or communication effect.

<a id="source-4efedf58f92b-16-requirements-for-tools"></a>

## 1.6 Requirements for tools

A reference that cannot be resolved, has the wrong target kind, or is ambiguous is an error. Tools MUST distinguish declaration paths, instance paths and local value paths even when their spelling is similar. A tool supporting rename MUST update declaration references and structural/member paths together, preserving the identity of each referenced occurrence.

---

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f"></a>

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-4-root-components-timing-cases-and-conformance"></a>

# 4. Root components, timing, cases and conformance

Normative chapter of the [SWAML 1.3 specification](#source-ffe5ff67da37).

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-41-root-components-and-profiles"></a>

## 4.1 Root components and profiles

The structural rules in this chapter apply to class and component roots/usages, and executable rules apply to operation and function members. Component specializes Class and Function specializes Operation; the category compatibility rules in chapters 1â€“3 remain in force. Both alternatives retain closed-root validation, private occurrence stores and explicit typed routes.

A root component is the closed occurrence selected for validation, deployment and execution. It uses the same component concept as reusable architecture: an inline `component Demo { â€¦ }` owns component children and connectors, while `component Demo: Assembly;` obtains them from a component definition. Several named roots in one package may instantiate the same definition independently or describe alternative assemblies. Root selection is explicit in the simulator/host by package-qualified name, and is not inferred from file order.

A typed root's optional body is a deployment binding only: deployments may reference existing instances; structural members and behavior MUST NOT be added there. An inline root may contain child components and its own private data, functions and ports. All paths in cases, deployment and simulation are relative to the selected root; the root name is not an extra child segment.

Instantiation recursively creates children and local variable storage, resolves delegation and connectors, checks closure, and then enables startup/periodic activation. No component may publish during a partially connected startup phase.

The root profile is `service`, `realtime` or `mixed`; omission means `service`. A root profile selects timing obligations independently of interface kinds: a `service` root may use any interface kind, and a `realtime` root may use a `service` interface. Only the service profile defaults; QoS, interface versions, timeouts and realtime obligations remain explicit:

| Obligation | service | realtime | mixed |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Explicit connector QoS | required | required | required |
| Function WCET/deadline/concurrency | optional | required for all used functions | required for all used functions |
| Connector maxLatency | optional | required | required |
| Sampled data maxAge | optional | required | required |
| Interface isolation and handler checks | required | required | required |
| Production scheduling evidence | target dependent | required for hard realtime claims | required for realtime claims |

The mixed profile is intentionally conservative: all used functions and connectors carry timing contracts. It does not infer that a cloud-facing function has no timing requirement. A future subassembly profile can relax this with explicit partitioning; no such implicit relaxation exists in 1.3.

A timing contract declares positive `wcet`, positive `deadline`, and `concurrency serial|reentrant`. WCET MUST be no larger than deadline. For periodic functions, deadline MUST be no larger than the period. A stateful function's timing contract applies separately to each run-to-completion reaction, including entry/exit/effect and any completion microsteps, not to the lifetime of the machine.

WCET is active CPU execution time for the activation including synchronous local helpers. Deadline is elapsed time from activation release to completion, including waiting and interference. Connector maxLatency excludes destination dispatch wait. For a chain of functions, the end-to-end bound must account for release delay, queue delay, interference, transport, WCET and response delay; simply summing WCET is insufficient.

A conforming validator checks arithmetic consistency. A conforming target backend must additionally prove or measure its declared timing assumptions with an appropriate execution model. The core specifies no rate-monotonic/EDF policy, task mapping, interrupt priorities, memory size, hardware access mechanism or clock synchronization algorithm. Such a backend MUST supply a named external deployment profile, and MUST NOT claim timing conformance without evidence for those obligations.

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-42-deployment"></a>

## 4.2 Deployment

`deploy instance.path on "sysml:Vehicle::ecu";` associates an existing component instance with an external system or part identity. `system:production::checkout` is another valid reference form. The quoted destination MUST be nonempty and use a URI-like scheme followed by a nonempty identity. The scheme and identity are preserved exactly for a host or target binding to resolve. SWAML does not declare a second hardware/system ontology or load external models through this statement.

A deployment references an instance, not a reusable definition. The root name may be used as its own deployment target, for example `deploy Demo on "system:production";`, to place root-owned behavior and establish its subtree default. If an immediate child has the same name as the root, using that ambiguous name as a deployment target is invalid (SW070). The same explicit instance path may occur in only one deployment. A component deployment applies to that instance and its descendants by default; a more specific descendant deployment overrides the inherited destination. Without a deployment on an instance or ancestor, its effective destination is Unallocated. A valid logical model may be unallocated; a target must supply and validate the placement needed for execution.

For `sysml:` references, the target integration must resolve the identified SysML part/system, preserve its external identity and establish allocation semantics. The string alone is not a SysML import, a validated cross-model link or evidence of SysML/KerML conformance. SWAML's parser checks its local path and reference form; the host checks external existence, kind, availability and execution suitability.

Deployment leaves interfaces, connector identity, handler ownership and private-data visibility unchanged. Serialization, addresses, credentials, task schedules and physical channels belong to the target binding. Every exported mapping must retain the SWAML instance identity and external destination.

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-architecture-and-target-scope"></a>

### Architecture and target scope

The same component and interface constructs describe embedded, service-oriented, web and cloud architectures. Choose each component boundary around a coherent responsibility with private state, functions and optional child components. External deployment references connect this software model to system architecture without embedding a hardware metamodel.

| Architecture | Core model | Target binding supplies |
| --- | --- | --- |
| AUTOSAR Classic | components for atomic software-component behavior; components for compositions; senderâ€“receiver and clientâ€“server contracts; periodic or input-triggered functions; MCU placement | software-component and port mapping, runnable/event mapping, RTE access semantics, task scheduling, ECU data representation and communication configuration |
| AUTOSAR Adaptive | application components; required/provided service contracts with methods and events; process placement | application and execution manifests, service-instance identities, discovery and availability policy, `ara::com` binding and transport configuration |
| Web / SOA | clients and services with explicit request, reply and notification routes; private service state; process/container placement | API and serialization mapping, endpoints, authentication, lifecycle, persistence and failure handling |
| Cloud services | independently placed services; explicit broker/storage adapters where they participate in the model; connector queues and retry contracts | broker and storage guarantees, infrastructure placement, resource limits, restart policy and replica routing |

These mappings are target obligations, not additional core syntax. A function is a behavior owner; mapping it to an AUTOSAR runnable or a web request handler requires an explicit activation and concurrency mapping. An external deployment reference alone does not establish that mapping. An interface's nominal version identifies its SWAML contract; it does not imply wire compatibility with a platform interface.

The 1.3 instance tree and connector topology are fixed for a selected root. Multiple replicas are separate authored occurrences with separate private stores. Discovery, replacement or scaling in a target system MUST preserve the modeled endpoint identities, routes and state isolation; changes to the modeled topology require a new root configuration. A deployment binding cannot introduce a new peer, a shared store or an unmodeled exchange.

The [architecture patterns](#source-153a44e6a38e) provide complete examples and explain the additional platform decisions for each mapping. The execution obligations are defined in [chapter 6](#source-34105487b7a4-67-execution-binding-contract).

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-43-requirements-and-cases"></a>

## 4.3 Requirements and cases

`requirement Name { doc /* obligation */ attribute id: string = "REQ-001"; }` declares a named obligation with optional formal documentation and immutable typed attributes. Requirements have no subject. Attribute names are unique; values use the ordinary nominal type rules and cannot read private state, functions or other attributes. `priority` is also accepted as an attribute name.

`satisfy Requirement of Design.path;` is a package-owned relation from a resolvable design element to a requirement. Sources may be software declarations or occurrences in the same package, but not requirements, cases, views or the package itself. Requirement references use the ordinary import/qualification rules. Duplicate source/requirement pairs are invalid.

`useCase Name of Root { ... }` describes intended use. `verificationCase Name of Root { verify Requirement; ... }` describes verification intent. Only verification cases own verify relations, before their exchanges; duplicate targets are invalid. Both kinds may own one doc and zero or more exchanges. Neither satisfaction nor verification links establish fulfilled status or execute a test. Evidence belongs to a versioned external simulation, test, analysis or review result.

A case step has a kind (`send`, `publish`, `call`, `reply`), two endpoint-member paths, a named connector at the selected root composition level, an optional earlier-step predecessor and a unique label. Case endpoints must be visible ports of the root itself or its immediate child components. A typed root resolves those children and connectors through its definition. Internal interactions at a deeper level require a separate root component assembled at that abstraction level. This limitation keeps case paths subject to the same hierarchy rule as structural connectors.

Both endpoints MUST identify the same member of the connector's interface. Senderâ€“receiver steps use data, publish steps use events, and request/reply steps use function signatures. A request follows client â†’ server or requires â†’ provides orientation; a reply reverses the matching request. A service publish goes from provides â†’ requires; ordinary pub/sub follows publisher â†’ subscriber.

`after label` imposes a happens-before dependency on an earlier declared step. No predecessor means no ordering is imposed relative to other steps. Source order is only a stable display tie-break, not an implicit total order. Thus unrelated notifications can remain concurrent in sv. A reply MUST have exactly one unanswered matching request among its transitive `after` ancestors, with reversed endpoints and the same connector/member. Zero or multiple candidates is an error (SW083); each request has at most one reply. A request may remain unanswered in a partial trace. A prior reply closes a candidate only when that reply is itself an ancestor of the current step. A reply on an independent causal branch cannot resolve ambiguity merely by appearing earlier in the file. Source order never chooses between ambiguous correlations. Realization by function bodies is a separate analysis claim requiring a witness; route-valid cases remain unverified until that evidence exists.

Cases describe representative traces, not exhaustive behavior. They do not execute a second program, introduce independent event definitions, or loosen function guards. A complete behavioral verification must check traces against function semantics and environmental assumptions.

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-relation-vocabulary"></a>

### Relation vocabulary

Subject-assignment relations consistently use `of`: a case of a root, a satisfaction claim of a design element, or a compact view of a subject. The relation does not change containment: cases, views and satisfaction relations remain package-owned, and satisfaction still points from design to requirement. Case `for` and satisfaction `by` remain invalid after the 1.2.1 correction; see the [migration guide](MIGRATION_1_2.md).

| Syntax | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `useCase C of Root` / `verificationCase C of Root` | Root context for routed case exchanges. |
| `satisfy R of Design.path;` | Design.path claims satisfaction of requirement R. |
| `view V iv of Subject;` / `include Subject;` | Select existing subjects for a projection. |
| `on` | Activation stimulus or external deployment location. |
| `as` | Local/routed call result or completion binding. |
| `:` / `=` | Type/header separator / value initialization or assignment. |
| `via` / `after` / `->` | Existing route / causal predecessor or timer / directed edge. |

`for` is used only by the counted-loop construct; it is unavailable for subject relations. The loop forms and their bounds are defined in chapter 3.

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-44-view-declarations"></a>

## 4.4 View declarations

A view declaration names a lower camel case view kind and one or more subjects in its own package. `view Name iv of Subject;` selects one subject. `view Name gv { include Subject; include Other recursive where kind == "function" and name != "debug"; }` selects a stable union. Each include resolves its subject before optional recursive expansion and filtering. Paths identify occurrences even when definitions are reused. Repeated paths are deduplicated in first-selection order. Empty results are valid; blocks require at least one include. Unknown or ambiguous paths are invalid. Filters use name, kind and path as strings; string/Boolean literals; ==, !=, and, or, not and parentheses. The whole predicate must be Boolean. Traversal is independent of filtering. Evaluation is pure presentation and cannot read stores or call behavior. gv and grv may reference any resolvable named semantic declaration or the owning package, except another view; iv requires a class/component root, definition or nested occurrence; cv references a case or root component, sv references a case or root component (for case selection), and fv/tv reference executable operations/functions. A view of an instance resolves through its definition while retaining the occurrence path and identity.

The seven source view kinds are gv, iv, cv, sv, grv, fv and tv. fv presents statement flow, interruptible regions or function-owned states. tv presents function timing. Both select executable operation/function declarations or occurrences; interface signatures, calculations and individual states are invalid subjects. A compact tv of a root or component definition is invalid. To select functions under that context, write an explicit block such as `view timeline tv { include App recursive where kind == "function"; }`. Selected results must all be executable operations/functions; no implicit kind filter or recursive expansion is added. Empty filtered results remain valid. Filters match exact authored kind: use `where kind == "operation" or kind == "function"` to select both executable categories. A timing selection does not require a trigger or declared timing contract and does not supply trace evidence.

View declarations carry no executable behavior. A tv presentation may reference an identified execution trace while keeping declared WCET/deadline/concurrency contracts separate from observed events. Missing traces or completions cannot be invented.

GV supports **Box in box** and **Tree** layouts of the same selected model. Box in box uses nested ownership frames; Tree uses an ownership hierarchy with explicit edges. Changing between them preserves declaration/occurrence paths, ownership, references, ports and routes. It does not instantiate, reparent or reconnect software. Layout, color and collapsed regions are presentation data. Authored include/filter clauses have the deterministic selection semantics above; they do not alter the selected model. The full [graphical contract](#source-5798f00f85a6) defines representation and source-edit invariants.

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-documentation-and-comments"></a>

### Documentation and comments

`doc /* multiline text */` is the owning declarationâ€™s formal specification. At most one is permitted. The grammar defines owner positions: first in a body, before a leaf semicolon, before a transition, or before a QoS policy body. Text is preserved literally, including line breaks and backslashes; blocks are non-nested and end at the first */ delimiter. Ordinary comments remain separate hidden lexical tokens: each // line and each /* */ block is one comment. Neither annotation affects execution. The lexical doc token includes its prefix and block; doc is included in the reserved-word inventory.

A comment belongs to the narrowest named declaration containing its source extent. A trailing comment on the same line as a completed declaration belongs to that declaration. File-level comments belong to the model. A formal doc belongs only to its grammar owner. Graphical tools draw each comment as a plain note inside the owner or connected with a plain dashed line. Formal doc is a labeled compartment or a table column; it must not be duplicated as a second owned specification. Hiding annotations never removes their source or ownership.

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-45-static-validity-rule-groups"></a>

## 4.5 Static validity rule groups

The following groups collectively define semantic validity. Diagnostic numbers identify the present implementation's related checks, not a license to omit unimplemented clauses.

| Group | Required checks | Diagnostic family |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reuse | acyclic single specialization; explicit compatible redefinition | SW014â€“SW015 |
| Names | pinned language edition, uniqueness, imports, reference target kind, ambiguity, lexical scopes | SW002â€“SW004, parser/linker |
| Contracts | interface kind/member compatibility, role compatibility, exact interface identity | SW010â€“SW013, SW031â€“SW032 |
| Values | nominal typing, arity, field completeness, enum membership, array shape, literal ranges, purity, no recursive value containment | SW020â€“SW028 |
| Architecture | acyclic specialization/composition, compatible explicit redefinition, local/immediate-child boundaries, one delegation, no duplicate links/aliases, closure/cardinality | SW030â€“SW035 |
| Time/QoS | positive/bounded values, explicit policy, sampled buffering, WCET/deadline/period, realtime completeness | SW040â€“SW045 |
| Behavior | local-only calls/data, outgoing provenance, signatures, unique handlers, return coverage, terminating error paths, bounded loops, no recursion | SW050â€“SW059 |
| States | legal stateful signature, final-state rules, priority, reachability, completion-cycle restrictions | SW060â€“SW064 |
| Allocation | existing instances, valid external destination identities, no duplicate exact deployments | SW070 |
| Requirements | immutable attribute typing, valid design sources, verification-case-only verify links, unique relations | SW090â€“SW091 |
| Presentation/cases | subject kind, connector/member paths, happens-before and uniquely correlated replies | SW080â€“SW083 |

<a id="source-1f8b7bb89e5f-46-conformance-levels"></a>

## 4.6 Conformance levels

**Syntax conformance (S):** the complete concrete grammar, pinned host/parser version, tokenization and AST construction. Recovery during editing must retain syntax diagnostics; an incomplete AST is not a valid model.

**Static conformance (V):** S plus every static validity rule in this specification, classified explicitly in [chapter 8](#source-7edf1a193e2a). A partial validator MUST enumerate checks that it lacks. It must not hide unresolved links or a validator crash as success.

**KerML mapping conformance (K):** V plus chapter 5's normalized elements and preserved SWAML semantic constraints, with a tested mapping against KerML 1.0 and its selected libraries. Export must retain a reverse trace map and stable identity. K does not automatically imply SWAML execution.

**Execution conformance (E):** V plus the operational, correlation, queue, timing-observation, failure and concurrency semantics. Target binding assumptions must be explicit. A simulator may abstract physical time while still reporting which timing obligations it does not check.

**Graphical conformance (G):** S and the graphical specification's projection and edit rules. G may present invalid models with diagnostics, but must not invent valid connectivity from layout. fv projects a function's statement flow or owned states; tv projects its timing with identified trace provenance and separate contracts. GV layout changes preserve model identity and topology.

The 1.3.1 revision introduces tv while retaining parser family 1.3 and simulation profile `swaml-reference/1.3`; exact reproduction also pins the package revision. See [ADR 0012](#source-a05b7d8f7938) and the [1.3.1 migration guide](#source-34d9e0d9ccf9).

A tool claiming `SWAML 1.3 S, partial V` accurately describes this repository's current core. It must not advertise K, E or G until those implementations and checks exist. A language host must require an explicit supported edition, and incompatible changes require documented migration. Version 1.1 is an intentional syntax revision; it is not backward-compatible with 1.0 despite the minor version number. Migration tooling must not change the meaning of models silently.

---

<a id="source-e4f9d1308ebf"></a>

<a id="source-e4f9d1308ebf-migrating-to-swaml-13"></a>

# Migrating to SWAML 1.3

The published 1.3.0 edition added loops, interruptible flow and function timing diagrams. This page records that migration; the [1.3.1 revision](#source-34d9e0d9ccf9) moves timing to the dedicated tv kind and introduces GV layout choices. Pin the host/parser to **1.3** and use the `swaml-reference/1.3` simulation profile. The current parser rejects requests for older or inferred editions; use an archived 1.2 release to reproduce its exact grammar.

<a id="source-e4f9d1308ebf-source-changes"></a>

## Source changes

Existing 1.2.1 constructs retain their meaning, including `of` relations, component ownership, copied values and explicit communication routes. The new reserved words in 1.3.0 were `for`, `forEach`, `do` and `interruptible`; rename earlier identifiers with those spellings. Files remain headerless. The new loops are optional; bounded while loops continue to work.

Use `forEach`, not two separate words. Counted for headers require one var declaration, Boolean condition and assignment update. while, for and do loops require an explicit bound. forEach uses its array length and does not accept an additional bound. The complete syntax and scope rules are in the [control-flow guide](#source-26fc5c2f4d44).

<a id="source-e4f9d1308ebf-interrupts-and-traces"></a>

## Interrupts and traces

An interruptible region adds no free event or communication route. Bind its handler to an incoming member of an owned typed port and keep exactly one receiving owner. Functions that declare regions must be triggered serial flow functions. Regions cannot be introduced into reentrant helpers or state actions as a substitute for their existing behavior rules.

Interrupted pending requests gain a terminal caller-side cancellation observation. Earlier server effects remain committed, and late responses remain correlated and discarded. Trace consumers must recognize regionEnter, regionExit, interrupt and requestCancelled events. Do not reinterpret old profile traces as if they contained these observations.

<a id="source-e4f9d1308ebf-timing-presentation"></a>

## Timing presentation

In the published 1.3.0 edition, timing diagrams were an FV mode with no separate view-kind keyword. The 1.3.1 correction uses tv for timing and keeps fv for flow, interruptible regions and states. Both select executable functions; a root/definition context requires an explicit recursive include filtered to functions. Migrate timing declarations and identifiers that conflict with the newly reserved tv word using the [1.3.1 guide](#source-34d9e0d9ccf9). Trace-backed diagrams continue to keep declared contracts separate from observations and preserve unfinished intervals. Parser family 1.3 and `swaml-reference/1.3` remain unchanged.

Published 1.2.0, 1.2.1 and 1.3.0 consolidated references, PDFs, manifests, checksums and release assets retain their versioned filenames. Development generation targets the separate 1.3.1 revision; it does not replace the published 1.3.0 evidence.

---

<a id="source-34d9e0d9ccf9"></a>

<a id="source-34d9e0d9ccf9-migrating-to-swaml-131"></a>

# Migrating to SWAML 1.3.1

Version 1.3.1 retains the headerless **1.3 parser family** and **1.3 reference simulation profile**. Pin the package revision when loading a model.

<a id="source-34d9e0d9ccf9-classes-and-operations"></a>

## Classes and operations

`class` is the general structural category and `component` its specialization. `operation` is the general executable/signature category and `function` its specialization. Existing component/function source retains its behavior. Both alternatives use the same ownership, isolation, typed communication, calculations, flow/states and execution contracts.

Use `class def` for a reusable class and `class name: Type;` for a static usage. A component definition may specialize a class or component; a class definition may specialize a class. A component usage needs a component definition; a class usage accepts either. Top-level class usages are closed execution roots, and typed root bodies still contain deployment bindings only.

Use `operation name(...)` wherever an executable member or interface callable signature is allowed. An operation signature accepts an operation or function implementation; a function signature requires a function implementation. Redefinition may narrow operation to function or class usage to component usage when the inherited contract remains compatible; widening is rejected.

View `kind` filters match exact authored categories. Select both executable categories with `where kind == "operation" or kind == "function"`. Both are valid fv/tv subjects; interface signatures remain descriptive contracts. `class` and `operation` become reserved, so rename colliding identifiers. See the [complete example](../examples/20-classes-and-operations.swaml) and [ADR 0014](#source-e41a024287b1).

<a id="source-34d9e0d9ccf9-service-interface-symbols"></a>

## Service interface symbols

clientServer and service ports now show a boundary square with a stem and ball for server/provides or socket for client/requires. senderReceiver and publishSubscribe retain their directional notation. Contract labels, routes, QoS, replies and service events remain explicit. This graphical revision requires no connector-source migration.

<a id="source-34d9e0d9ccf9-timing-views"></a>

## Timing views

Use `tv` for a function timing view. Keep `fv` for statement flow, states and interruptible regions.

```text
view polling tv of Demo.first.poll;

view timeline tv {
  include Demo recursive where kind == "function";
}
```

These fragments assume the existing `Demo` architecture in [example 17](../examples/17-function-timing.swaml). A compact timing subject must be an executable operation/function declaration or occurrence. A compact component root is invalid: select its descendant functions explicitly with `recursive` and `where`. Ordered union, filters, empty results and occurrence identity follow the existing view-selection contract. For observed timing, the host binds an identified run and concrete occurrence identities separately from the source view declaration.

`tv` is now reserved. Rename an existing identifier spelled `tv` and update its references. A 1.3.0 host rejects the new view keyword; source files do not silently select a parser version.

<a id="source-34d9e0d9ccf9-operator-and-test-inputs"></a>

## Operator and test inputs

Remove `on manual`. Bind an application entry point to a named, typed event with `on receive port.member`. Declare the event in a `publishSubscribe` or `service` interface, give its source and receiver owned ports, and connect them through an explicit route. A local helper instead omits the trigger and is called only within its component.

The simulator no longer accepts the `manual` JSON option. It rejects direct function releases and has no replacement destination-event injection option. Author an ordinary driver function that publishes the event using `on start`, `on every`, or a finite state/timer sequence. A host may generate that source; it must validate and preserve it with the run evidence. See [events and external inputs](#source-a75326096d8c) and [ADR 0013](#source-7ec66a39d535).

Inputs now traverse their declared connectors, so observed activation times include input transport and queue delay. Update recorded traces, source hashes, activation identities and fault-offer ordinals. A recursive timing selection also includes driver functions; use explicit function selections when focusing a Timing View on application behavior.

`manual` is no longer reserved as an identifier. Its former trigger syntax remains invalid.

<a id="source-34d9e0d9ccf9-structure-layouts"></a>

## Structure layouts

Existing `gv` declarations keep their meaning. Choose **Box in box** or **Tree** in the viewer. Both layouts show the same ownership paths. Save the choice in a presentation sidecar if persistence is needed; no model-source migration or new layout keyword is required.

Published [1.3.0 release notes](RELEASE_1_3_0.md) describe the earlier FV timing presentation. The [current decision](#source-a05b7d8f7938) records the revised view taxonomy.

---

<a id="source-a75326096d8c"></a>

<a id="source-a75326096d8c-events-and-external-inputs"></a>

# Events and external inputs

Represent operator commands, test inputs and asynchronous notifications as named interface events. Declare their payload types, publish them from an owned function, and receive them through a typed port and explicit route.

```swaml
package OperatorEvents {
    interface publishSubscribe Commands version "1.0.0" {
        event adjust(desired: int);
    }
    component def Controller {
        port commands: Commands subscriber;
        var setting: int = 20;
        function adjust(desired: int) on receive commands.adjust {
            setting = desired;
        }
    }
    component Demo {
        port operatorCommands: Commands publisher;
        component controller: Controller;
        connect input: operatorCommands -> controller.commands qos {
            delivery atMostOnce; ordering fifo; capacity 4; overflow reject;
        };
        function driveInputs() on start {
            publish operatorCommands.adjust(22);
        }
    }
}
```

The root component acts as the test driver in this small model. Its `driveInputs` function publishes `adjust(22)`; the connector delivers a copied `int` value to `Controller.adjust`. A larger architecture can place the operator, device adapter or test driver in a separate component with its own publisher port.

`on receive commands.adjust` names the exact incoming event. The same trigger form handles data reception. `on call` implements an interface function; an omitted trigger defines a local helper. `on start` and `on every` describe local lifecycle and periodic behavior.

For a one-shot delayed input, use a driver function with an initial waiting state and an `on after` transition to a final state whose entry publishes the event. Multiple timed states form a finite test sequence. The [operator lifecycle model](../examples/04-stateful-function.swaml), [function timing model](../examples/17-function-timing.swaml) and [interrupt model](../examples/19-interruptible-function.swaml) demonstrate authored event schedules.

The simulator executes the authored driver; it has no direct function-release or destination-event injection option. Operator and test messages therefore use the same interfaces, queues, delivery policy and transport timing as other messages. A host-generated driver remains ordinary source that is validated and recorded with the run. Use cases and verification cases describe intent; they do not generate inputs or prove a test result.

The [1.3.1 migration guide](#source-34d9e0d9ccf9) explains removal of `on manual`; [ADR 0013](#source-7ec66a39d535) records the design.

---

<a id="source-7ec66a39d535"></a>

<a id="source-7ec66a39d535-0013--event-driven-operator-and-test-inputs"></a>

# 0013 â€” Event-driven operator and test inputs

**Status:** accepted for the 1.3.1 revision Â· **Date:** 2026-10-18

<a id="source-7ec66a39d535-decision"></a>

## Decision

Remove the `on manual` function trigger and the simulator's `manual` release option. Operator commands, test inputs and device notifications are named, typed events in `publishSubscribe` or `service` interfaces. A receiving function uses `on receive port.member`; its signature matches that member's payload. States and interruptible regions use the same routed inputs.

An operator, device adapter or test driver is represented by ordinary component-owned behavior. Its functions publish the declared events through owned ports and explicit connectors or delegation. These messages retain the normal route queues, delivery policy, copying, latency, ownership and cancellation rules. There is no direct harness activation of a function, destination-port injection or implicit event source.

Reproducible simulations author a driver using existing `on start`, `on every` or function-owned states with `on after` transitions. A finite state schedule can emit several named events at specified times and then terminate. Local helper functions may organize a source component's publications; they remain local calls. No extra scheduling keyword or driver element enters the software ontology.

A host that generates a test schedule must generate ordinary SWAML source, include it in the validated workspace and preserve it with the run evidence. Generating a driver does not grant access to private behavior or bypass topology closure. The portable simulator accepts resource limits, horizon and transport faults; model stimuli are authored behavior.

<a id="source-7ec66a39d535-migration-and-evidence"></a>

## Migration and evidence

This updates the unpublished 1.3.1 candidate. Parser family and reference simulation profile remain 1.3; exact reproduction pins the package revision and source hashes. Published historical editions retain their syntax and artifacts.

Replace each former manual entry point with a named incoming event and an explicit emitting function/route. Where a function is only an internal helper, omit the trigger and call it locally from its owner. Remove old `manual` JSON configuration; the simulator rejects it rather than silently ignoring it. It likewise does not accept a replacement destination-event injection option.

Positive tests demonstrate typed payloads, finite schedules, independent occurrence stores, queueing, timeouts and interruptions through real routes. Negative tests reject the retired trigger/API and retain type, ownership, routing and parser-recovery diagnostics. Timing evidence includes input transport and driver activations; a Timing View selects its displayed function occurrences explicitly.

---

<a id="source-a05b7d8f7938"></a>

<a id="source-a05b7d8f7938-0012--dedicated-timing-view-and-structure-layouts"></a>

# 0012 â€” Dedicated timing view and structure layouts

**Status:** accepted for 1.3.1 Â· **Date:** 2026-10-18

<a id="source-a05b7d8f7938-decision"></a>

## Decision

`tv` names the Timing View. `fv` presents function statement flow, states and interruptible regions. This supersedes the timing-as-FV presentation in [ADR 0011](#source-8ce404340af5); its execution and cancellation rules remain in effect.

A Timing View selects executable operation/function declarations or occurrences using the same stable selection, union and filter rules as other views. Every selected element must be an operation or function. To select descendant functions from a component context, use explicit recursive expansion and filtering. Empty filtered results remain valid. No implicit expansion or filtering occurs. The host associates observed timing with an identified trace and concrete occurrence identities; the view declaration does not start execution. Declared timing contracts and observed activation spans remain separately labeled.

The General View, `gv`, supports **Box in box** and **Tree** layouts. Nested boxes express component ownership through containment. A tree expresses the same ownership through explicitly labeled parent-to-child edges. Both preserve definition versus occurrence identity, full occurrence paths, direct function ownership and independent private stores. External deployment targets remain references outside the software ownership hierarchy. Tree edges do not imply communication routes or activation order.

Layout is presentation metadata. It does not introduce a second structure view kind or source keywords. Switching layouts preserves semantic selections and identities.

<a id="source-a05b7d8f7938-compatibility-and-evidence"></a>

## Compatibility and evidence

The parser family and reference simulation profile remain 1.3. Pin the package revision as well. `tv` becomes a reserved keyword in 1.3.1, so an existing identifier spelled `tv` must be renamed. Authored timing views migrate from `fv` to `tv`; flow and state views remain `fv`. Published 1.3.0 assets retain their original contents.

Positive and negative language tests cover compact and block timing selections, occurrence identity, filters, recovery and invalid subjects. The showcase demonstrates both structure layouts and dedicated timing navigation. Trace-backed timing is evidence about the identified reference run; it does not establish physical execution duration or schedulability.

---

<a id="source-cd440bd1250a"></a>

<a id="source-cd440bd1250a-swaml-131-release-notes"></a>

# SWAML 1.3.1 release notes

**Edition:** 1.3.1 Â· **Date:** 2026-10-18 Â· **Parser family:** 1.3 Â· **Reference simulation profile:** 1.3

- Add general `class` classifiers and `operation` behavior/signatures. `component` specializes `class`; `function` specializes `operation`. Preserve the authored category through definitions, usages, views and mapping, with compatible narrowing and separate private stores.
- Use ball/socket notation for clientServer and service ports: server/provides use a ball, client/requires use a socket. Typed routes, contract versions, QoS and reverse replies/events retain their meaning.
- Reassess UML notation and semantic transformation with the expanded categories. No XMI adapter is included.

- Add `tv`, a dedicated timing view selecting executable function declarations or occurrences. `fv` continues to present statement flow, states and interruptible regions.
- Add Box in box and Tree presentations of `gv`, preserving the same ownership hierarchy and occurrence identities.
- Remove `on manual` and direct simulator function releases. Operator and test inputs use named, typed events published by ordinary driver functions through explicit routes.
- Align the showcase, examples, keyword and notation inventories, and specification with the revised view taxonomy.

Read the [migration guide](#source-34d9e0d9ccf9) before upgrading: `tv` becomes reserved, authored timing views change from `fv` to `tv`, and the old manual trigger/configuration is rejected. Drivers use the existing lifecycle, event and timer semantics; input transport now contributes to the demonstrated timing observations. Declared contracts remain distinct from observed timing evidence.

`class`, `operation` and `tv` are newly reserved. Existing component/function models retain their behavior; rename colliding identifiers before migrating. Read [ADR 0014](#source-e41a024287b1) and the [UML transformation assessment](#source-2d2e1a9ea4f7). Published 1.3.0 reference files and release assets are preserved.

---

<a id="source-26fc5c2f4d44"></a>

<a id="source-26fc5c2f4d44-loops-interrupts-and-function-timing"></a>

# Loops, interrupts and function timing

SWAML 1.3 adds three loop forms and interruptible regions. FV presents flow, states and interruptible regions. The dedicated TV presents function timing from the same component-owned behavior model. The 1.3.1 development correction retains parser family and simulation profile 1.3; see the [migration guide](#source-34d9e0d9ccf9) and [ADR 0012](#source-a05b7d8f7938).

<a id="source-26fc5c2f4d44-choose-a-loop"></a>

## Choose a loop

| Form | Test and bound | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `while (condition) bound n { â€¦ }` | Test first; zero or more iterations; explicit bound. | Fresh body locals each iteration. |
| `for (var i: int = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) bound 3 { â€¦ }` | Initialize once; test first; update after body; explicit bound. | Header variable remains inside the loop. |
| `forEach (item: int in values) { â€¦ }` | Visit each element of one copied fixed-size array. | Fresh typed element and body locals each iteration. |
| `do { â€¦ } while (condition) bound n;` | Execute at least once, then test; explicit bound. | Body locals are not visible in the condition. |

The spelling is `forEach`, following lower camel case keywords. Conditional bounds must be positive safe-integer literals. If a condition is still true after the permitted number of body executions, execution fails before an extra iteration. A return or failure exits the enclosing function/frame. There are no break, continue, range shorthand or unbounded loops.

```swaml
package LoopGuide {
    component Demo {
        var total: int = 0;
        function run() on start {
            for (var i: int = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) bound 3 {
                total = total + i;
            }
            forEach (value: int in [1, 2, 3]) {
                total = total + value;
            }
            do { total = total + 1; } while (total < 10) bound 2;
            assert total == 10;
        }
    }
}
```

The complete [loop model](../examples/18-control-loops.swaml) also demonstrates nested loops and mutation of the original array during snapshot iteration.

<a id="source-26fc5c2f4d44-interrupt-a-protected-region"></a>

## Interrupt a protected region

Use an owned input port member to interrupt a named region:

```text
function run() on start {
    interruptible work {
        call job.perform() as value within 50 ms
            otherwise { fail "Job failed"; };
        status = value;
    } on receive control.stop {
        status = 2;
        why = reason;
    }
    // Both normal completion and the interrupt handler continue here.
}
```

Here control.stop is an event with a string parameter named reason. A sender reaches it through the ordinary typed connector; an external actor label cannot deliver it directly. The [complete interrupt model](../examples/19-interruptible-function.swaml) includes the contracts, sender, server, routes and a simulation configuration.

The handler replaces the remaining protected work. It retains the same activation and deadline; it does not run concurrently with the protected body. Previous changes remain committed. If a request is pending, its continuation is cancelled and its later reply cannot resume the function. The server may still finish and retain its effects. Input arriving before entry or after exit is explicitly discarded.

The reference example starts a request at 0 ms, commits the server effect at 5 ms, receives the stop event at 7 ms, completes the interrupt handler at 7 ms and discards the late reply at 10 ms. The final worker status is 2 and the server's committed count remains 1.

Interruptible regions require a triggered serial flow function. Receive transitions remain the stateful-function mechanism. Nested regions are supported with distinct interrupt members; an outer interrupt unwinds the active inner region. A protected region can call helpers, but helpers cannot declare their own interrupt endpoint. Handler payload bindings cannot shadow enclosing values.

<a id="source-26fc5c2f4d44-read-a-tv-timing-diagram"></a>

## Read a TV timing diagram

Select **Timing View (tv)** in the showcase. The [timing model](../examples/17-function-timing.swaml) provides two independent clients and servers, including repeated activations. Four trace scenarios show delayed success, timeout with a late reply, an observation horizon and a deadline stop. Select a function occurrence or activation to inspect its events and source.

Select one function with `view observed tv of Demo.first.poll;`. The timing model's block explicitly selects both client and server function occurrences; its root-owned input driver remains in the complete trace. To include every descendant function, including a driver, use `view allTiming tv { include Demo recursive where kind == "function"; }`. Compact component-root/definition subjects are invalid. Timing contracts and triggers are not required merely to select a function. A view declaration does not run it.

In the success run, driver publications occur at 0, 0 and 1 ms. Input transport releases the client activations at 2, 2 and 3 ms. Repeated `first.poll` activations complete at 6 and 10 ms; the second waits for its serial owner between 3 and 6 ms. The timeout run shows the first timeout at 32 ms and late reply at 36 ms, with a queued activation still open at the explicit 40 ms observation horizon. The separate deadline scenario stops at 49 ms.

| Drawing | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Release and dispatch markers | Activation readiness and the start of its dispatch. |
| Ready interval | Waiting before dispatch, including serial backlog. |
| Running marker or interval | Observed active phase; zero-cost steps retain zero duration. |
| Request-wait interval | Suspension while the caller awaits its correlation. |
| Completion, failure or interrupt marker | The observed lifecycle event for that activation. |
| Open interval at the boundary | Still unfinished when the trace stops or its horizon is reached. |
| Declared deadline marker | Absolute release-plus-deadline contract boundary. |

WCET, deadline and concurrency are shown separately as declared values. Waiting time is not CPU execution time. A bounded logical-time trace supplies observations, not physical WCET measurement, production schedulability or a universal verification result. Trace identity, profile, selected root and source digest remain inspectable.

The portable `projectFunctionTiming` API consumes trace evidence and optional occurrence contracts. It retains source ordinals, activation/correlation identity, cancellation and late responses without mutating the supplied evidence. The checked-in showcase projection is regenerated with `node scripts/build-timing-demo.mjs` and verified by the normal language gate.

---

<a id="source-8ce404340af5"></a>

<a id="source-8ce404340af5-adr-0011--loops-interruptible-flow-and-function-timing"></a>

# ADR 0011 â€” Loops, interruptible flow and function timing

Status: accepted for SWAML 1.3.0. Date: 2026-10-18.

The FV timing presentation below records the 1.3.0 decision. [ADR 0012](#source-a05b7d8f7938) supersedes that presentation with dedicated `tv` timing views in 1.3.1. The control-flow, interrupt and timing execution rules remain in effect.

<a id="source-8ce404340af5-control-flow"></a>

## Control flow

Add counted `for`, copied-array `forEach` and post-test `do â€¦ while` alongside `while`. Conditional loops require an explicit positive safe-integer literal bound. `forEach` is bounded by its fixed array length and accepts no second bound. These forms preserve copied values, lexical scope, range checks and statement-boundary scheduling.

The counted header contains one `var` initializer, one Boolean condition and one assignment update. The initializer executes once; the condition executes before each iteration; the update executes after a normally completed body. Header variables exist only in the header and body. Each body iteration has a fresh child scope. A `forEach` evaluates and copies its array once, then supplies a fresh copied, typed element to each iteration. Changes to the source array or element local do not alter that snapshot. A do loop executes its body before its first test; body locals are not visible in its condition.

<a id="source-8ce404340af5-interruptible-regions"></a>

## Interruptible regions

`interruptible work { â€¦ } on receive control.stop { â€¦ }` defines a named protected region and one interrupt handler. The endpoint is an incoming data/event member of a typed port owned by the same component. The function owns the receiving behavior; delivery uses the existing explicit route and queue. There is no global interrupt or new architectural participant.

Regions are limited to triggered serial flow functions. Stateful functions use their receive transitions; reentrant functions and untriggered helpers cannot declare regions. Helpers called from a protected body can be unwound. Region names are unique in their function, and an interrupt member cannot also be an activation trigger or another region's interrupt member. Existing single-handler and delegation rules still apply.

A region is armed from entry to normal exit or interruption. Available input outside that interval is discarded with an explicit undeliverable-interrupt observation. Delivery selects the active region for its member, disarms it, and transfers control at a statement boundary to the handler. Nested regions unwind when an outer region is interrupted. The handler sees enclosing values and copied payload bindings, but cannot see protected-body locals. After normal handler completion, execution continues after the region.

Interruption retains the activation identity, serial lock, snapshot and deadline. It cancels an outstanding caller-side request continuation, including its otherwise branch, and makes a still-pending correlation terminal with an Interrupted reason. It does not cancel server execution or roll back effects. A late reply is discarded. Existing terminal outcomes remain immutable: timeout wins its correlation race at expiry, even if a later interrupt transfers control before the queued failure continuation runs. Already scheduled continuations cannot resume the unwound stack.

Return and failure still terminate their function/frame and disarm regions. A region does not catch ordinary runtime failures. Interrupt dispatch bypasses ordinary activation dispatch only to resume its existing serial activation; it creates no parallel handler activation. This is cooperative model-level cancellation, with no claim of processor-interrupt latency or physical preemption.

<a id="source-8ce404340af5-function-timing-diagrams"></a>

## Function timing diagrams

Timing is a further FV projection, alongside statement flow and function-owned states. Keep the existing view kinds. A timing projection shows selected function occurrences, activation identities, release/dispatch, ready/running/request-wait phases, completion/failure, interruption and correlated late responses on a common logical-time axis.

Declared WCET, deadline and concurrency remain distinct contract values. Observed bars come from an identified trace with model/run provenance. Zero-cost simulation execution remains a zero-duration marker; it must not become a measured CPU duration. Horizon or stopped runs retain open intervals, and missing completion is not invented. Reused definitions and concurrent activations keep distinct lanes. Layout cannot create scheduling or verification evidence.

<a id="source-8ce404340af5-edition-and-evidence"></a>

## Edition and evidence

The new keywords can conflict with earlier identifiers. The current parser and simulator therefore select language/profile 1.3 explicitly; archived 1.2 releases retain their grammar and evidence. Source migration is described in the 1.3 guide.

Acceptance covers positive and malformed parsing, scope/type/bound failures, observable loop stores, interruption of suspended and nested execution, timeout ties, duplicate/inactive input, independent occurrence identities and reproducible timing projections. The canonical catalog and interactive showcase use source-backed contexts. Target bindings still supply physical scheduling and cancellation-latency evidence.

---

<a id="source-ba515b0190e9"></a>

<a id="source-ba515b0190e9-adr-0007--components-functions-and-calculations"></a>

# ADR 0007 â€” Components, functions and calculations

Status: accepted for SWAML 1.2.0. Date: 2026-10-16.

[ADR 0010](#source-3df772169cbd) records the 1.2.1 relation-syntax correction and its migration.

The software vocabulary uses nested components, functions for executable behavior, calculations for pure values and deployment references to an external system model. This decision supersedes the component/unit split and operation terminology in ADR 0001 and the corresponding restrictions in ADR 0004. Historical editions retain their original syntax and semantics.

<a id="source-ba515b0190e9-ownership"></a>

## Ownership

A component is the software boundary for private state, typed ports and functions. It may also contain child components, connectors and delegation. Leaf and composite components use the same rules. Each instance has independent state and activation locks; containment does not permit direct access to a child's private values or functions. Local calls stay inside the owning component, while every cross-component exchange follows a typed port/interface/member route.

An executable `function` owns statements or directly owned states. Interface `function` signatures remain separate contracts whose handlers bind explicitly. As refined by [ADR 0008](#source-9b006ba52eb1), calculations belong only to functions. A `calc` declares typed `in` inputs and one named non-void `return` expression. Calculations use copied inputs and other local calculations; they cannot read mutable state, communicate or call executable functions. The separation makes guards and value expressions effect-free without requiring an architectural participant for every calculation.

The input/result presentation draws on the calculation concepts in [SysML 2.0, clause 7.19](https://www.omg.org/spec/SysML/2.0/Language/PDF). SWAML's calculation is a deliberately bounded, original language rule; the similar vocabulary does not establish SysML syntax compatibility or kernel conformance.

<a id="source-ba515b0190e9-reuse"></a>

## Reuse

`component def` defines a reusable type, and `component name: Type;` creates one concrete instance. `specializes Base` permits one base definition. `redefines` explicitly replaces an inherited member with a compatible member of the same category. Specialization and effective containment must remain acyclic. Local references in inherited behavior resolve against the effective specialized component, while every instance receives distinct storage.

Compatibility preserves ports, private value types, function signatures, triggers, concurrency and body category. Function timing cannot be weakened. A redefined child may use its original type or a specialization. Connectors and delegations retain endpoint paths. Unmarked inherited-name collisions are errors. This is controlled architectural reuse; a separate class construct, dynamic creation/destruction, reference payloads and multiple inheritance are outside the contract.

<a id="source-ba515b0190e9-deployment-and-types"></a>

## Deployment and types

`deploy controller on "sysml:Vehicle::ecu";` links a component instance to an external system/part identity. General `system:` identities are also supported. SWAML validates the local path and reference form; a host resolves the external model and establishes platform suitability. The core does not define hardware nodes or claim an implemented SysML importer/exporter.

The value vocabulary is bool, int, uint, byte, real, string, result-only void, nominal records/enums and fixed arrays. byte and uint have checked bounded integral domains. Numeric conversion must preserve values or report a range failure; it must not wrap or truncate silently.

<a id="source-ba515b0190e9-migration-and-evidence"></a>

## Migration and evidence

This change intentionally breaks earlier 1.2 candidate and 1.1 source syntax. Update source, model fixtures, public guides, diagrams, keyword/mapping inventories and the reference simulation profile together. The selected language edition remains 1.2 because this is an unpublished candidate revision, not a silent change to a published edition. Profile `swaml-reference/1.2` records the new execution representation and function-based harness inputs. The [migration guide](MIGRATION_1_2.md) supplies the conversion steps.

Acceptance requires parser and semantic negative cases, inherited behavior and independent-store traces, calculation purity, numeric bounds, external deployment checks, reproducible figures and current documentation fixtures. Source reuse, a bounded simulator run and an external identity string make no claim of production schedulability, universal verification or cross-language conformance.

---

<a id="source-9b006ba52eb1"></a>

<a id="source-9b006ba52eb1-adr-0008--function-local-calculations-and-one-call-syntax"></a>

# ADR 0008 â€” Function-local calculations and one call syntax

Status: accepted for SWAML 1.2.0.

Calculations are local to functions. Local and routed calls share `call`, while private and local mutable values share `var`. This refines [ADR 0007](#source-ba515b0190e9).

Components own private data, ports, children and executable functions. Each function declares its calculations first, followed by flow or states. A calculation resolves only in its enclosing function, including state guards and action blocks. Its expression sees only explicit copied inputs, constants and sibling calculations. It cannot capture mutable data or call executable behavior. Redefining a function replaces the complete body and calculation scope; calculations have no independent specialization or redefinition.

Local flow functions may return values. `call helper(args) as result;` binds a copied, typechecked value to a fresh caller local. void local calls omit the binding. The helper shares the caller's activation lock and snapshot, and a failure unwinds the local call stack without undoing earlier effects. Triggered and stateful functions cannot be called locally. Inherited calls select the effective function on the concrete component instance; calculation references remain lexical to the selected function.

`call port.member(args) as result within D otherwise { â€¦ };` preserves explicit typed RPC routes, correlation, timeout and failure handling. Case RPC steps also use `call`. Local and routed calls share one keyword while retaining distinct AST nodes and execution rules. Executable functions cannot appear in expressions.

`var` declares both private component storage and mutable block locals. Ownership determines lifetime and visibility. Live-value shadowing remains forbidden. The retired `invoke`, `request` and `let` spellings require migration and receive no compatibility fallback.

The reference parser, validator and simulator must cover result copying/ranges, lexical calculation isolation, inherited dispatch, state guards, malformed inputs and unchanged routing/failure behavior. This change adds no dynamic object construction, implicit cross-component access, production scheduling proof or external-language conformance.

---

<a id="source-1a801412d435"></a>

<a id="source-1a801412d435-requirements-documentation-and-views"></a>

# Requirements, documentation and views

Capture an obligation as a requirement. Add only the attributes you need, write its formal specification in `doc`, and connect the design and verification intent explicitly.

```swaml
package Example {
    requirement Ready {
        doc /* The application shall initialize its result to 2. */
        attribute id: string = "REQ-001";
        attribute priority: uint = 1;
    }
    component App {
        doc /* Initialize application state. */
        // This value belongs to App.
        var result: int = 0;
        function initialize() on start {
            result = 2;
            assert result == 2;
        }
    }
    satisfy Ready of App;
    useCase Start of App {
        doc /* A user starts the application. */
    }
    verificationCase CheckStartup of App {
        doc /* Run startup and inspect the final result: it must equal 2. */
        verify Ready;
    }
    view coverage gv {
        include Ready;
        include App;
        include Start;
        include CheckStartup;
    }
    view behavior fv {
        include App recursive where kind == "function" and name == "initialize";
    }
}
```

| Concept | What it records | How it is displayed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| requirement | An obligation with typed attributes and optional formal text | Flat rectangle with attributes and doc compartments; one row in a requirements table |
| doc | One formal specification owned by a declaration | A labeled doc compartment or a doc table column |
| ordinary comment | One line or block of informal source explanation | A plain rectangular note inside its owner, or joined to it by a plain dashed line |
| satisfy | A design element claims to fulfill a requirement | Dashed arrow from the design element to the requirement, labeled satisfy |
| useCase | Intended use in a selected root context | Case frame labeled useCase, with optional routed exchanges |
| verificationCase | A verification procedure or scenario | Case frame labeled verificationCase; verify arrows point to requirements |

`doc /* ... */` preserves multiline text literally; string escape sequences are not interpreted. Write it first inside a declaration body. For a leaf such as a port, put it before the semicolon: `port input: Values receiver doc /* Receives the sensor sample. */;`. A declaration can have one doc and any number of ordinary comments. Each `//` line and each `/* ... */` block is separate. Blocks are not nested. A word `doc` inside an ordinary comment or string stays ordinary text.

Attributes are immutable metadata, not variables. They use the same value types as the software model, including records, enums and fixed arrays. Their expressions cannot read private data, call functions or capture other attributes. Requirements have no mandatory subject, identifier attribute, priority or verification method. `priority` is allowed as an attribute name even though it is also a transition keyword.

Use `satisfy` for the design and `verify` for verification intent. A use case cannot verify a requirement. A verification case may describe a manual inspection in its doc, a route-valid sequence of exchanges, or both. These declarations do not run a test or prove the requirement. Record actual simulation, test, analysis or review results separately with the model and tool version that produced them.

A compact view remains valid: `view wiring iv of App;`. A view block combines `include` entries. Each entry selects its subject, optionally its descendants with `recursive`, and optionally filters those candidates with `where`. Predicates use `name`, `kind` or `path`, string/Boolean literals, equality/inequality and Boolean combinations. Filters do not prune traversal: excluding a parent by name does not hide matching descendants. Repeated selections are deduplicated by occurrence path, in first-selection order. An empty result is valid; an empty block is not.

Common kinds are `component`, `function`, `requirement`, `useCase`, `verificationCase`, `port`, `interface`, `variable`, `calculation` and `state`. Names and paths are exact and case-sensitive. All selections resolve in the view's package. Each selected element must fit the view kind. A view never selects another view.

See the [complete example](../examples/16-requirements-and-views.swaml), [normative rules](#source-1f8b7bb89e5f), [graphical catalog](#source-2d93a6cc8eab) and [interactive showcase](https://voidaliot.github.io/swaml/#traceability).

---

<a id="source-f700391cc2a8"></a>

<a id="source-f700391cc2a8-adr-0009--requirements-documentation-and-selected-views"></a>

# ADR 0009 â€” Requirements, documentation and selected views

Status: accepted for SWAML 1.2.0. Date: 2026-10-16.

The relation spellings in this historical decision are superseded in 1.2.1 by [ADR 0010](#source-3df772169cbd): current satisfaction and case contexts use `of`.

Requirements have no subject. Declarations support one formal documentation block, separate lexical comments, two case kinds and composable view selections. This decision replaces the earlier string-only requirement, generic case and case-to-requirement satisfies clause.

`requirement` owns immutable typed `attribute` values and optional `doc /* ... */` text. It has no subject, behavior or private runtime store. `satisfy Requirement by Design.path;` records a design claim. `verificationCase` owns `verify Requirement;` links; `useCase` describes intended use. Neither relation establishes evidence by itself. Both case kinds share the existing routed, partially ordered exchange semantics.

Formal documentation is raw multiline text, attached to its declaration, with at most one block per owner. Ordinary line and block comments remain separate lexical annotations. They have no declared identity or executable effect. The source projection retains every comment and its owner; graphical notes and formal documentation compartments remain distinct.

The lexer consumes `doc` and its block as one DOC_COMMENT token. This makes multiline documentation unambiguous with hidden ordinary comments and preserves text exactly. The keyword inventory explicitly includes this lexical keyword. Unterminated blocks are syntax errors. The grammar identifies every permitted owner and position; body-bearing declarations put documentation first, leaf declarations put it before the terminating semicolon, and transitions use a prefix.

All reserved words use lower camel case. Primitive type words are `bool`, `byte`, `uint`, `int`, `real`, `string`, `void`; view kinds are `gv`, `iv`, `cv`, `sv`, `grv`, `fv`. Identifiers remain case-sensitive and may use capitals. Browser is a navigation surface, not a source keyword.

View blocks contain one or more `include` selections. Optional `recursive` expansion preserves occurrence paths; optional `where` predicates combine typed `name`, `kind` and `path` comparisons with `and`, `or`, `not` and parentheses. Selections form a stable union. Filters select model elements; they cannot execute model behavior or introduce communication routes.

The conceptual comparison is with the [official SysML 2.0 language](https://www.omg.org/spec/SysML/2.0). SWAML deliberately defines its own smaller syntax and semantics. This release does not add SysML interchange or kernel validation.

---

<a id="source-3df772169cbd"></a>

<a id="source-3df772169cbd-adr-0010--consistent-subject-relations"></a>

# ADR 0010 â€” Consistent subject relations

Status: accepted for SWAML 1.2.1. Date: 2026-10-18.

Subject-assignment relations consistently use `of`. The 1.2.0 grammar used case `for`, satisfaction `by` and compact-view `of` for analogous references. This patch corrects those spellings without changing their semantic targets, ownership or execution. Any future use of `for` is limited to loop syntax.

<a id="source-3df772169cbd-decision"></a>

## Decision

Use `useCase Name of Root { ... }`, `verificationCase Name of Root { ... }`, `satisfy Requirement of Design.path;` and `view Name iv of Subject;`. View blocks continue to use `include` to select multiple subjects. A case's root context is a reference; the package still owns the case. Satisfaction still points from the design element to the requirement. Imports, qualified targets, duplicate checks and occurrence identity are unchanged.

The remaining relation vocabulary denotes different concepts: `on` selects an activation stimulus or deployment location; `as` binds a call result or completion; `:` denotes typing/header structure; `=` initializes or assigns values; arrows denote direction; `via` selects a route; `after` denotes a causal predecessor or timer. These constructs do not receive a general-purpose `of` alias.

The only current loop is bounded `while (...) bound n`. There is no `for` loop production in 1.2.1. The word `for` has no relation meaning; any future language use must be a separately specified loop construct. Removing `for` and `by` from current keyword coverage reflects the actual grammar, not an implementation of new loop syntax.

<a id="source-3df772169cbd-migration-and-version-selection"></a>

## Migration and version selection

Version 1.2.1 intentionally changes source compatibility after the 1.2.0 publication. The model meaning is preserved, but the parser rejects old case `for` and satisfaction `by` forms. Migration is contextual: change those delimiters to `of`, while preserving strings, ordinary prose, triggers, deployment locations and result bindings.

Hosts currently select the 1.2 language family. The package/release revision determines its concrete parser, so reproducible consumers must pin both. Use the archived 1.2.0 release to reproduce its earlier grammar. Published 1.2.0 Markdown, PDF, manifest, checksum, tag and release assets remain preserved. New artifacts have 1.2.1 filenames.

<a id="source-3df772169cbd-audit-and-evidence"></a>

## Audit and evidence

The audit covers grammar keywords, semantic reference roles, keyword and notation inventories, current examples, source-backed showcase projections and maintained guides. It also corrects outdated descriptions of requirements, root-owned case endpoints, view selection unions and the published release status. Regressions cover positive `of` references, imported/qualified roots and requirements, alias duplicate satisfaction, rejected old spellings and malformed input. Existing simulations check that execution traces and stores remain unchanged.

---

<a id="source-84e06ed0ba86"></a>

<a id="source-84e06ed0ba86-swaml-ontology-and-flat-views"></a>

# SWAML ontology and flat views

SWAML 1.3.1 models software with **Class â†’ Component** and **Operation â†’ Function** category specializations, plus pure calculations. Classes/components own child usages, private data, typed ports and executable operations/functions. Executable members expose statement flow or states; calculations produce pure values from explicit inputs. Start with the [graphical overview](#source-8da46ae3c9ac), then use this inventory alongside the [normative specification](#source-ffe5ff67da37). The arrows here run from general to specialized category; they do not allocate objects or link runtime instances.

<a id="source-84e06ed0ba86-ownership-and-reuse"></a>

## Ownership and reuse

```text
Class / Component occurrence
â”œâ”€ Child class/component occurrences, recursively
â”œâ”€ Ports, connectors and boundary delegation
â”œâ”€ Private values
â””â”€ Operations / Functions
   â”œâ”€ Statement flow OR states and transitions
   â””â”€ Calculations
      â””â”€ Typed inputs â†’ pure expression â†’ named output
```

A package organizes names; it is not an extra software layer. `component def` declares a reusable type. `component child: Type;` creates an instance with separate state. A top-level component selects the root architecture. Definitions may specialize one base, with compatible inherited replacements marked `redefines`. Specialization is reuse of declarations, not shared runtime storage.

| Concept | Owns or contains | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Package | Imports and declarations | Namespace for definitions, contracts and views. |
| Class / Component definition | Children, ports, private data, operations/functions and connections | Class is general; component is specialized. One compatible base. |
| Component instance | Effective members and separate private state | One use of a definition with a distinct path. Leaf and composite instances follow the same rules. |
| Root component | Inline members or one definition instance; deployment bindings | Closed architecture selected for execution. Omitted profile means service. |
| Operation / Function | Inputs/result, trigger, timing, local calculations and statements or states | Operation is general; function is specialized. Omitted result means void. |
| Calculation | Typed inputs and one named non-void output expression | Pure function-local computation; cannot read mutable values or communicate. |
| State / transition | Entry/exit blocks / guard, trigger, priority and effect | Control owned directly by one function. |
| Private variable | Type and pure initializer | Mutable copied-value storage inside one component instance. |
| Value type | Primitive domain, record fields, enum literals or fixed array extent | bool, byte, uint, int, real, string, result-only void and structured values. |
| Interface | Typed data, events or function signatures | Nominal versioned communication contract. Signatures and implementations have distinct owners. |
| Port | Contract, role and optionality | An owned boundary endpoint. |
| Connector | Typed endpoints and complete policy selection | Route between owned or immediate-child ports; its queues are independent. |
| Delegation | Outer and immediate-child port references | Boundary alias with the same contract and role; introduces no queue or handler. |
| QoS policy | Complete delivery, ordering, capacity, overflow and optional bounds | Reusable values selected explicitly on each connector. |
| Deployment | Instance path and external identity | Link to a host-resolved system or SysML part, with nearest-ancestor inheritance. |
| Requirement | Immutable typed attributes and one optional formal doc | Named obligation without a subject. Design elements may claim satisfaction; verification cases may record verification intent. |
| Use / verification case | Root-context reference, optional doc and routed exchanges; verify links only in a verification case | Intended use or verification intent. Claims and cases do not establish evidence. |
| Documentation | One formal specification attached to an eligible declaration | Ordinary comments remain separate lexical notes. |
| View | Kind and one or more ordered subject selections | Projection of existing model identities, with optional recursive expansion and pure filtering. |

The same component may implement an external contract and contain collaborating children. Local behavior cannot bypass those children's interfaces. An inherited function resolves local names against its specialized component's effective members, while another instance keeps its own state. The [specialization example](../examples/15-component-specialization.swaml) demonstrates this distinction.

<a id="source-84e06ed0ba86-communication-contracts"></a>

## Communication contracts

| Interface kind | Members | Role pair | Direction |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| senderReceiver | Sampled/queued data | sender / receiver | Data travels sender â†’ receiver. |
| publishSubscribe | Events with typed payloads | publisher / subscriber | Events travel publisher â†’ subscriber. |
| clientServer | Function signatures | client / server | Request client â†’ server; correlated result/failure returns. |
| service | Function signatures and events | requires / provides | Request requires â†’ provides; result/failure and provider events return. |

`Settings::Panel` is a definition. `Settings::Cabin.front.panel` and `Settings::Cabin.rear.panel` are two instances with distinct `displayed` values and function activations. `Cabin.frontLink` and `Cabin.rearLink` may select the same LocalLink policy while retaining separate queues. The [service setting model](../examples/08-service-field.swaml) supplies these exact identities.

<a id="source-84e06ed0ba86-how-the-views-look"></a>

## How the views look

Browser is the navigation surface. gv, iv, cv, sv, grv, fv and tv are the seven declared view kinds. Every drawing uses flat geometry, explicit labels and stable source identities. Color supports grouping; it never carries the only semantic distinction.

| Surface | Subject | Flat appearance and purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Browser | Workspace or selected subtree | Indented ownership tree with distinct definitions, inherited members and instance paths. |
| gv â€” General | Package or named semantic element | Box in box ownership frames or a Tree of the same selected identities, with child/data/function details and labeled type, specialization and deployment references. |
| iv â€” Interconnect | Class/component root, definition or occurrence | Owner/child cards, boundary ports with service balls/sockets, named typed connectors and boundary delegation. Locally handled ports remain visible. |
| cv â€” Case | Case or root | Use/verification cases show their root context and routed exchanges. satisfy arrows run from design elements to requirements; verify arrows run only from verification cases. |
| sv â€” Sequence | Case or root case selection | Participant headers, lifelines and typed messages with explicit dependencies and correlated replies. Vertical layout adds no ordering. |
| grv â€” Grid | Package or named semantic element | Filterable rows for identities, owners, contracts, functions, policies, external destinations and evidence. |
| fv â€” Flow | Executable operation/function | Owned statement flow with decisions, bounded loops, calls and interruptible regions, or directly owned states with guarded/timed transitions. |
| tv â€” Timing | Executable operation/function | Function occurrence/activation lanes on a time axis, with observed phases from an identified trace and separate declared contracts. |

Box in box and Tree are presentation choices within gv. Switching preserves the same occurrence paths, ownership and typed topology; it does not create another component tree or change communication routes.

A compact tv selects one function. To select functions under a root or reusable definition, use an explicit block such as `view timeline tv { include App recursive where kind == "function"; }`. A root itself, signature, calculation or state is not a tv subject. No trace evidence is implied by declaring a view.

External deployment targets are labeled reference cards, not software children. A `sysml:` string remains an unresolved external reference until the integration host supplies validation. The [graphical notation](#source-5798f00f85a6), [canonical catalog](#source-2d93a6cc8eab) and [worked examples](#source-49c5dd8a67d1) define the detailed symbols.

<a id="source-84e06ed0ba86-start-small"></a>

## Start small

Begin with an inline root and the components that represent useful private-state boundaries. Add reusable definitions and specialization when they help express a real family of architectures. Keep a calculation local when it only transforms values. Add a typed port and route whenever work crosses a component boundary.

Use gv/iv for responsibility and communication, cv/sv for intended interactions, grv for contract review, fv for function flow/states and tv for timing. The showcase supports inspection and layout; arbitrary graphical source editing, production timing proof and cross-language export require the additional implementations listed in [implementation status](#source-f25fc4312adb).

The dedicated tv kind and GV layouts are documented in the [1.3.1 migration guide](#source-34d9e0d9ccf9) and [ADR 0012](#source-a05b7d8f7938). The parser family and execution profile remain 1.3.

<a id="source-84e06ed0ba86-requirements-and-formal-documentation"></a>

## Requirements and formal documentation

Requirements own typed attributes and one optional `doc /* ... */` specification. Design elements link through `satisfy`; verification cases link through `verify`. Use cases describe intent. Ordinary comments remain independent notes. View kinds use lower camel case, and view blocks combine multiple selections and Boolean filters. See the [guide](#source-1a801412d435) and [complete model](../examples/16-requirements-and-views.swaml).

---

<a id="source-386496547756"></a>

<a id="source-386496547756-swaml-for-uml-architects"></a>

# SWAML for UML architects

SWAML 1.3 supports familiar structural and behavioral modeling with explicit software boundaries. Start with classes or components and their contracts, compose named usages, then inspect operations/functions and interaction. Component specializes Class; Function specializes Operation. Both pairs retain the same bounded ownership and execution rules. The reference for UML terminology is [OMG UML 2.5.1](https://www.omg.org/spec/UML/2.5.1). The correspondences below are modeling guidance, not a UML import format or conformance claim.

The [UML transformation reassessment](#source-2d2e1a9ea4f7) distinguishes current notation from semantic conversion. Flat class/component frames, operation/function compartments and clientServer/service ball/socket are SWAML notation. No UML importer, exporter or XMI adapter is implemented or proposed by this release. [ADR 0014](#source-e41a024287b1) records the language hierarchy.

<a id="source-386496547756-translate-the-modeling-concepts"></a>

## Translate the modeling concepts

| Familiar UML concept | SWAML representation | Boundary to keep in mind |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Package | `package` and explicit imports | Package declarations form namespaces; imports are not transitive. |
| Class / component type | `class def` / `component def` | Component specializes Class; both own composition, ports, private values and operations/functions. |
| Part / typed occurrence | `class name: Type;` / `component name: Type;` | Class accepts either definition kind; component requires component type. Every usage has separate private state. |
| Generalization / redefinition | `specializes` and `redefines` | Single specialization with compatible member replacement; class cannot inherit component, and component/function kinds cannot be widened by redefinition. |
| Interface | Versioned interface with member signatures | Exact nominal identity; structural similarity does not establish compatibility. |
| Provided / required interface | Ball at server/provides; socket at client/requires, attached to typed ports | Applies to clientServer and service; reverse replies/events retain their route. Other kinds keep their own directed notation. |
| Assembly connector | Named `connect` between child ports | Select a complete QoS policy; the arrow identifies the contract orientation. |
| Delegation connector | `delegate outer -> child.port` | Same role and contract; no conversion, additional queue or independent behavior. |
| Local operation implementation | Untriggered operation/function invoked with local `call` | Calls remain inside the owning component; parameters and results are copied. |
| Provided operation implementation | Operation/function bound with `on call port.member` | Signature and implementation have distinct owners; function signatures require function handlers, while operation signatures permit either kind. Remote calls need a typed route. |
| Signal reception | Interface event and `on receive port.member` | Events are contract members; there are no free signals or hidden event buses. |
| Activity | Operation/function statement body, shown in fv | Structured conditionals and bounded loops; no fork/join tokens or activity allocation. |
| State machine | States directly inside an operation/function, shown in fv | Flat states, entry/exit, guarded transitions; no orthogonal or hierarchical regions. |
| Interaction / sequence diagram | Exchanges in `useCase` or `verificationCase`, shown in sv | Explicit partial order and routes; a case is not automatically executed as a test. |
| Use case | `useCase Name of Root` and Case View (cv) | Describes intended use of one root, with optional routed exchanges; actors, include and extend are absent. |
| Verification case | `verificationCase Name of Root` and explicit `verify` links | Records verification intent and optional exchanges; links do not establish passed verification. |
| Datatype / enumeration | `datatype` / `enum` | Copied nominal values; neither classes nor references to remote objects. |
| Deployment | External system/part references in `deploy` bindings | Allocation metadata does not prove physical schedulability. |

The diagram labels `Â«component defÂ»`, `Â«componentÂ»` and `Â«interfaceÂ»` name SWAML concepts. They do not declare UML stereotypes. Rectangular compartments, typed names, square boundary ports, explicit connectors, sequence lifelines, decisions and states provide a familiar reading vocabulary. All drawings are flat. The [notation chapter](#source-5798f00f85a6) defines the exact vocabulary.

<a id="source-386496547756-choose-the-interface-by-interaction"></a>

## Choose the interface by interaction

| Architectural need | Kind and roles | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Retain individual accepted values | senderReceiver: sender â†’ receiver; queued member | [Greeting](../examples/01-hello.swaml) |
| Retain the latest pending measurement | senderReceiver: sender â†’ receiver; sampled member | [Controller](../examples/02-realtime-controller.swaml) |
| Call a function and await a result | clientServer: client â†’ server | [Health check](../examples/07-client-server.swaml) |
| Publish typed events to explicit subscribers | publishSubscribe: publisher â†’ subscriber | [Audit publication](../examples/03-service-pubsub.swaml) |
| Expose functions and notifications together | service: requires â†’ provides | [Temperature setting](../examples/08-service-field.swaml) |

The [interactive interface reference](../showcase/README.md) displays all five patterns from these exact sources. A source/target arrow is not a statement that all traffic is one-way: RPC replies return on the correlated route, and service events travel from provides to requires. Sampled data is a queued value transfer with capacity one and dropOldest, not shared-memory access. The full direction and cardinality rules are in [chapter 2](#source-410a83e3a0f0).

Separate contracts when parts need different membership, policies or lifecycles. An interface containing sampled and queued members applies the same capacity-one policy to both. A consumer of a service must implement all of its incoming events; there is no implicit partial implementation. Use an adapter component to translate between contracts and versions.

<a id="source-386496547756-build-the-model-incrementally"></a>

## Build the model incrementally

1. Declare value types and interface signatures. Contract-only packages are valid reusable libraries.
2. Define component boundaries and implement their handlers and emitters. Every incoming member needs one handler owner; an empty implementation stub is not evidence of behavior.
3. Compose components into reusable component definitions. Expose boundaries through same-role delegation. A library definition can leave ports awaiting its eventual context.
4. Instantiate an inline or typed root. Closure is checked for each concrete root, including expanded library parts. Every nonoptional port must be connected; optionality does not waive handlers or provenance.
5. Select a named complete QoS policy for each connector. Choose delivery and overflow deliberately; reliability does not establish handler success or exactly-once effects.
6. Add cases and views to explain the architecture. Add deployment and timing when the target question requires them; distinguish declared bounds from execution evidence.

Begin with [the complete greeting model](../examples/01-hello.swaml), then compare [reusable policy syntax](../examples/10-reusable-qos.swaml). Headerless files require an explicitly selected 1.3 parser/host. Reproducible runs also pin the package revision; revision 1.3.1 retains parser family 1.3. The [user guide](#source-c9e79bd9c348) contains validation commands.

<a id="source-386496547756-read-browser-and-the-seven-views"></a>

## Read Browser and the seven views

| Surface | Architectural question | UML reading aid |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Browser | What is declared, owned or instantiated? | Model explorer; definition and occurrence paths are separate. |
| gv | Which parts compose this architecture? | Structural overview and containment. |
| iv | Which contracts connect the parts? | Ports, assembly and delegation. |
| cv | Which use and verification cases describe this root? | Case intent and explicit verification claims; narrower than UML use-case modeling. |
| sv | Which exchanges depend on which others? | Lifelines and typed messages; visual height alone adds no order. |
| grv | What are the exact properties and bindings? | Model table and property inspection. |
| fv | How does this operation/function execute? | Structured flow or a flat state machine owned by the function. |
| tv | What timing is declared or observed for this operation/function? | Declared contracts are separate from observations in an identified execution trace. |

<a id="source-386496547756-choosing-the-vocabulary"></a>

## Choosing the vocabulary

Use `class` for a general software type or usage and `component` when the specialized architectural boundary is intended. Both may own private data, child usages, ports and executable behavior. Use `operation` for general executable behavior and `function` for its specialized kind. Both may own calculations, flow or states; both use local calls and typed remote routes. Function does not mean pure: use `calc` for a pure input-only expression.

A component definition may specialize a class or component; class definitions specialize classes only. An operation may be redefined as a compatible function, and a class usage may be narrowed to a compatible component usage. Reverse widening is invalid. The authored kinds remain visible in diagrams, Browser and properties.

View kind filters are exact. To select both executable alternatives, use `kind == "operation" or kind == "function"`; to select both structural alternatives, use `kind == "class" or kind == "component"`. IV accepts both structural kinds, and FV/TV accept both executable kinds.

<a id="source-386496547756-deliberate-language-limits"></a>

## Deliberate language limits

SWAML classes have separate stores and explicit single ownership. They do not add shared heap objects, association classes, arbitrary multiplicities, dynamic creation, generics or cross-owner attribute access. A usage declaration denotes one occurrence; repeated usages are explicit declarations. Class instances remain distinct from copied `datatype` values. All communication between owners retains ports, nominal interfaces and explicit routes.

Operations and functions share serial locks, snapshots, copied parameters/results, event provenance and timing obligations. Named `qos def` reuse, default service profiles and omitted void results reduce repetition. Existing component/function models retain their meaning; the new vocabulary does not silently import UML token flow, object references or unrestricted OCL. The [implementation matrix](#source-f25fc4312adb) separates current validation and simulation from future graphical authoring, KerML export and UML transformation.

---

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7"></a>

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-uml-notation-and-transformation-reassessment"></a>

# UML notation and transformation reassessment

SWAML 1.3.1 includes class/component and operation/function vocabulary, flat class compartments and clientServer/service ball/socket notation. **Component specializes Class; Function specializes Operation.** These are semantic kinds in one SWAML model, with shared ownership, port boundaries and execution rules. See [ADR 0014](#source-e41a024287b1).

This chapter reassesses UML-to-SWAML and SWAML-to-UML transformations against that language. It defines a decision table, not an import/export implementation. No XMI adapter is included or proposed by this release. The baseline is SWAML revision 1.3.1, parser family 1.3, and [OMG UML 2.5.1](https://www.omg.org/spec/UML/2.5.1/). Familiar notation does not establish UML conformance or executable equivalence.

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-reading-the-compatibility-table"></a>

## Reading the compatibility table

The **drawing** column compares the current SWAML notation with familiar UML symbols: **reuse** preserves a familiar symbol, **adapt** retains SWAML labels or a changed shape, and **omit** has no corresponding semantic relation. Notation coverage is distinct from a complete graphical editor.

The **UMLâ†’SWAML** column describes semantic feasibility: **subset** has a bounded mapping, **configured** needs explicit choices, and **outside core** needs redesign or external preservation. No row claims an implemented transformation. A future conversion would need source identities and an explicit account of every restriction or loss. The reverse direction is assessed separately below.

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-structure-contracts-and-values"></a>

### Structure, contracts and values

| UML element | SWAML drawing and target | UMLâ†’SWAML conditions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Package | **Reuse:** tabbed namespace frame; `package`, Browser/gv | **Subset:** flatten nested namespaces with an explicit identity/name map. SWAML imports expose a whole directly imported package and are not transitive; selective imports, aliases and visibility need normalization. |
| Component | **Reuse:** flat rectangle, component marker and compartments; `component def`, gv/iv | **Configured:** preserve the Component kind, which specializes Class. Choose the software boundary explicitly. UML realizing classifiers and indirect instantiation do not automatically identify a SWAML runtime component. Retain the SWAML definition marker. |
| Class | **Reuse:** flat class frame with `class def`, typed private compartments and operations/functions, gv | **Subset:** map an owned software class to Class; map an explicitly value-only type to datatype instead. SWAML Class has separate usage stores, owned ports and copied values; UML shared references, public object access, unrestricted dispatch and heap semantics do not follow from the spelling. |
| Composite part / property | **Reuse:** nesting; alternatively an explicitly labeled ownership edge in Tree, gv | **Subset:** one owned composite part becomes one child class/component usage. Shared aggregation, references, subsets, optional parts and variable object multiplicities do not map to single ownership. UML destruction semantics are not imported. |
| InstanceSpecification / object | **Adapt:** `name : Type` occurrence box, gv | **Configured:** static class/component usages only, with a selected complete root and one compatible type. Usage declarations cannot override private initializers; differing source slot values need explicit generated specializations or an initialization redesign. Typed root bodies permit deployment bindings only. Partial slots, shared objects, multiple classifiers and dynamic creation need redesign. Do not underline ordinary SWAML occurrences as if they were all UML InstanceSpecifications. |
| Generalization | **Reuse:** hollow triangle pointing to the base; `specializes`, gv | **Subset:** acyclic single inheritance. Component may specialize Class or Component; Class may specialize Class only. Multiple inheritance, generalization sets and abstract-class semantics have no direct mapping. |
| Redefined member | **Adapt:** explicit `redefines` badge and inherited-member reference | **Subset:** retain the member family and compatible contract. Operation may narrow to Function and Class usage to compatible Component usage; reverse widening is invalid. UML covariance or an inferred override is insufficient. |
| Interface | **Reuse:** interface rectangle with member compartment, iv | **Configured:** select one nominal contract, explicit version and interaction kind. Similar signatures alone do not establish contract identity. |
| Provided / required interface | **Reuse:** ball at server/provides and socket at client/requires, attached to owned ports for clientServer/service, iv | **Configured:** retain the role, contract and version. Service notifications still need explicit reverse-event semantics. Ball/socket does not represent senderReceiver or publishSubscribe. |
| Port | **Reuse:** square on the owning class/component boundary, iv | **Configured:** one interface and one valid role per port. Multiple provided/required contracts, conjugation and protocol-state semantics cannot be silently collapsed. |
| Assembly connector | **Adapt:** named connector between owned typed ports, iv | **Configured:** establish exact endpoints, direction, cardinality and a complete QoS policy. A UML line does not supply SWAML delivery, order, capacity or overflow values. |
| Delegation connector | **Reuse:** outer port to immediate child port, iv | **Subset:** same contract and role; no conversion, extra queue or new behavior. |
| Association / shared aggregation | **Omit:** no generic communication or ownership arrow | **Outside core:** a reference or open diamond cannot become a connector or child implicitly. An explicit redesign must establish ownership or a typed communication contract. |
| Dependency / realization | **Adapt:** a reference overlay only when backed by a mapped reference or interface implementation | **Configured:** a generic dependency has no executable meaning. Interface realization requires actual member implementation through mapped ports; it is not component inheritance. |
| Attribute | **Reuse:** typed private-value compartment, gv/grv | **Subset:** private initialized value with a compatible copied type. Public/protected/static/shared/reference-valued or read-only fields need explicit redesign; do not silently change their access or mutability. |
| DataType / Enumeration | **Reuse:** typed record or enum compartment, gv/grv | **Subset:** finite nominal copied values. Recursive records, object references, datatype operations and unbounded collections have no direct mapping. Map primitive ranges explicitly: UML Integer is not automatically SWAML's bounded `int`. |
| Multiplicity | **Adapt:** one explicit class/component occurrence; fixed extents only for copied values | **Subset:** a part has multiplicity one. Repeated components need separate usage declarations; object collections are not copied fixed arrays. Optional ports are independent of optional objects. |

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-functions-events-and-behavior"></a>

### Functions, events and behavior

| UML element | SWAML drawing and target | UMLâ†’SWAML conditions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Operation / method | **Reuse:** operation signature and implementation compartment; `operation` or specialized `function`, gv/fv | **Subset:** distinguish interface signature, local untriggered implementation and routed `on call port.member` implementation. Operation signatures accept either compatible implementation kind; Function signatures require Function. Input parameters and at most one result; overloads, out/inout parameters and multiple results need an explicit contract redesign. |
| Query operation | **Adapt:** class/component operation or function; locally owned `calc` only when its stronger rules hold | **Configured:** UML `isQuery` can still read object state. A SWAML calculation must be pure, input-only, operation/function-local and have exactly one named typed non-void result. |
| CallOperationAction | **Reuse:** labeled call action, fv | **Subset:** a local call remains inside the owning component. A remote call needs an interface route, timeout and failure branch. Arbitrary object-target calls and asynchronous RPC calls have no direct mapping. |
| Signal / Reception | **Adapt:** event signature and named receive activation, iv/fv | **Configured:** put the signal in an explicitly selected interface and map its publisher, receiving owner, ports and routes. Preserve payload names/order/types. No free events or implicit bus. |
| SendSignalAction / AcceptEventAction | **Adapt:** publication and receive labels, fv/sv | **Subset:** sends require an emitting operation/function. An entry reception may become `on receive`; arbitrary mid-flow waiting is not equivalent to activation. Interrupt reception needs the exact interruptible-region rules. |
| Activity / action | **Reuse:** rounded action boxes and control arrows in an operation/function-owned fv | **Subset:** supported structured sequential statements only. UML activity token semantics, object flows, performer lanes and arbitrary opaque bodies are not supplied by the drawing. |
| Decision / merge | **Reuse:** diamond with guarded alternatives, fv | **Subset:** structured `if`/`else` with valid Boolean expressions; no general token merge. Preserve branch scope and order. |
| LoopNode / ExpansionRegion | **Adapt:** loop frame with visible bound or copied array extent, fv | **Subset:** bounded `while`, `for`, `do` or copied fixed-array `forEach` when the source structure fits. Unbounded loops and parallel expansion require redesign. |
| Fork / join | **Omit:** no parallel bars in function flow | **Outside core:** do not serialize concurrent branches and claim preserved behavior. Separate activated components/functions require an explicit architectural redesign. |
| InterruptibleActivityRegion | **Adapt:** current explicit protected-region frame, fv | **Configured:** SWAML cancels the same serial activation at statement boundaries. Committed effects and server work survive; a UML interrupt edge alone does not establish these semantics. |
| State / transition | **Reuse:** rounded state, initial circle and labeled transition, fv | **Subset:** one flat operation/function-owned machine, started with `on start`. Preserve supported entry/exit effects, receive/after/completion triggers, explicit priorities and timer origins. |
| FinalState | **Adapt:** named SWAML state rectangle with final badge, fv | **Configured:** UML FinalState cannot own entry behavior; SWAML final states can. A bare UML bullseye would hide that behavior and the state identity. Preserve completion at the function scope. |
| Composite / orthogonal / history states | **Omit:** no corresponding state shapes | **Outside core:** hierarchy, history, deferred events, doActivity and submachines cannot be flattened silently. |
| Concurrency flags | **Adapt:** explicit SWAML function concurrency labels | **Configured:** UML operation concurrency is not automatically SWAML owner-wide serial exclusion or reentrant snapshot semantics. Reentrant SWAML operations/functions cannot mutate component state. |
| OpaqueBehavior / constraint / OCL | **Adapt:** source text or mapped Boolean expression at an explicit evaluation point | **Configured:** only a defined expression/statement subset can execute. Preserve other bodies in the conversion report; do not replace them with empty functions or claim arbitrary OCL support. |

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-cases-timing-documentation-and-deployment"></a>

### Cases, timing, documentation and deployment

| UML element | SWAML drawing and target | UMLâ†’SWAML conditions |
| --- | --- | --- |
| UseCase | **Adapt:** SWAML case frame for `useCase`, with `of Root` context, cv | **Subset:** map narrative and supported exchanges to a selected root. Keep verification cases visually distinct; `include`/`extend` have no direct relations. |
| Actor | **Adapt:** optional presentation glyph tied to an explicitly mapped class/component | **Configured:** an interacting actor needs a class/component with owned ports and named events/functions. An actor association supplies no stimulus: execution needs an owned emitting function, typed member and explicit route, plus an authored driver schedule when applicable. Do not infer actors from names or create a second participant identity beside the component. |
| Interaction / message | **Reuse:** occurrence lifelines, filled call heads, open event/data heads and dashed replies, sv | **Subset:** actual typed routes, supported case steps, explicit causal predecessors and unique reply correlation. Visual height cannot invent total order. Current canonical SWAML uses filled non-reply heads; a style change must update its inventory and fixtures together. |
| CombinedFragment / general ordering | **Adapt only when exact order is representable** | **Configured:** a case step has at most one named predecessor. Multiple-predecessor joins and alt/loop/par/critical/assert/neg fragments cannot be silently linearized. Cases describe exchanges; importing one does not implement a function. |
| Timing diagram | **Adapt:** shared time axis, intervals and event markers, tv | **Configured:** declared operation/function timing contracts can be shown without a trace. Observed intervals/events require trace provenance and remain separate from those contracts; UML state lifelines or constraints alone are not execution traces or measured WCET. |
| Comment | **Adapt:** plain linked note, retaining lexical ownership | **Configured:** map the UML comment's owner to one SWAML lexical owner; its annotated-element references are separate from ownership. Preserve additional annotation targets externally. Ordinary comments remain separate from the one formal `doc`. Keep the accepted flat plain-note convention. |
| Formal specification text | **Adapt:** owned doc compartment and table field | **Configured:** select at most one formal document per owner. Do not promote all UML comments to `doc`. |
| Requirement / satisfy / verify | **Adapt:** existing SWAML requirement/case notation | **Configured profile mapping:** these are not plain UML-core requirement constructs. Recognize an explicit SysML or testing-profile mapping; only verificationCase owns `verify`, and neither relation proves its claim. |
| Node / Artifact / Deployment | **Adapt:** flat external-target reference and deployment binding, gv | **Configured:** UML deployment allocates artifacts; map artifacts to software occurrences explicitly, then bind to host-resolved external identities. Preserve node/hardware models externally. No cuboids or hardware ontology enter SWAML. |
| Profile / stereotype | **Adapt:** explicitly mapped metadata only | **Configured:** recognize supported profiles by identity. Unknown stereotypes do not generate new keywords, execution rules or claims; retain them in the report for review. |

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-coverage-of-uml-diagram-families"></a>

## Coverage of UML diagram families

Several UML diagrams can project the same SWAML model. They do not require additional SWAML view kinds.

| UML diagram family | SWAML surface | Projection scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Package | Browser / gv | Namespace navigation; imports retain SWAML scope. |
| Class | gv / grv | Class/component definitions, owned operations/functions and copied value types. |
| Object | gv | Static class/component usages with distinct paths/stores. |
| Component | gv / iv | Composition and typed software contracts. |
| Composite structure | gv / iv | Single-owned parts, ports, assembly and delegation. |
| Deployment | gv | External target references and software bindings only. |
| Profile | Conversion metadata | Recognized profile mappings; no new core view. |
| Use case | cv | Root-scoped use cases and separate verification coverage. |
| Activity | fv | Restricted structured operation/function flow. |
| State machine | fv | Flat operation/function-owned states. |
| Sequence | sv | Typed case exchanges and their exact supported partial order. |
| Communication | sv / iv | Exchanges or topology when actual routes are known. |
| Interaction overview | Browser / case navigation | Links between cases; no implicit executable composition. |
| Timing | tv | Declared function contracts, optionally accompanied by trace-backed observations. |

Browser and grv remain useful navigation/table surfaces without needing UML diagram labels. The gv Box in box and Tree layouts retain identical selected occurrence paths and topology.

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-reassessment-by-transformation-direction"></a>

## Reassessment by transformation direction

The added vocabulary removes forced Class-to-Component and Operation-to-Function renaming. It does not remove the semantic restrictions below. A future transformation is acceptable only for an explicitly selected supported subset, with identity and loss reporting. Diagram positions and repeated drawings never create new semantic identities.

| SWAML construct | SWAMLâ†’UML representation | Fidelity boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Class / Component definitions and usages | Corresponding classifiers and owned composite properties; preserve authored kinds | Preserve one owner, multiplicity one, acyclic composition and separate usage stores. Do not turn copied value records into shared objects. |
| Operation / Function | UML Operation with a mapped method/behavior; preserve specialized Function kind in constrained metadata | UML has no automatic equivalent of SWAML Function's specialized kind. Preserve typed inputs/result, body category, trigger and local calculation scope. |
| Class/component single specialization and member redefinition | Generalization and explicitly linked redefined features | Preserve the legal subtype directions and full SWAML contract compatibility; a broader UML override is insufficient. |
| Typed ports, nominal interface and connector | Ports, interfaces, provided/required ball/socket and assembly/delegation | Preserve exact version and member identities, direction, cardinality, complete QoS and resolved routes. Ball/socket alone cannot preserve service reverse-event obligations. |
| Private values and copied types | Private properties, data types and enumerations | Constrain bounded numeric domains, fixed array extents, copied payloads and separate stores. Plain UML numeric names or reference properties do not establish these rules. |
| Local calculations | Explicit pure expression behavior scoped to the owning executable | Preserve typed inputs, one named non-void output and the ban on mutable captures or executable calls. Do not relabel an arbitrary UML query as a calculation. |
| Flow and flat states | Supported activity/state subset with explicit execution constraints | Preserve bound failure, serial locks, snapshots, state timers, interrupt cancellation and committed effects. A general UML activity/state diagram permits behaviors outside this subset. |
| Use/verification cases and ordered exchanges | Use cases and restricted interactions with explicit intent metadata | Preserve root context, exact partial order and correlated replies. Verification cases, satisfy and verify need a declared profile/interpretation; the links are not proof. |
| TV timing | Timing presentation plus identified source observations and separate contracts | Retain run/model/profile identities, activation IDs, ordinal ordering, open intervals and declared-versus-observed values. A declared bound is not a trace. |
| Deployment | Software occurrence-to-external-target reference, with explicit artifact correspondence | Preserve external system/part identities and resolution status. Hardware models remain outside SWAML. |

A semantic transformation must distinguish **directly represented**, **represented with explicit constraints/metadata**, **requires an explicit modeling choice**, and **unsupported/redesigned** elements. Dropping source material or choosing a default is not a lossless conversion. Complete source and target identity maps, all chosen policies, unsupported constructs and retained external information would be required evidence.

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-acceptance-boundaries"></a>

## Acceptance boundaries

A converted contract/value library or behavior-free structural subset may validate without an executable architecture. A port-owning architecture additionally needs actual handler and emitter behavior. Missing behavior remains a diagnosed draft; an invented empty body cannot establish an equivalent model. For accepted source, ordinary pinned SWAML parsing, linking, validation and concrete-root closure remain mandatory.

A reverse transformation must preserve the SWAML constraints in a declared, inspectable interpretation. A UML consumer that ignores those constraints can read structural content, but cannot claim equivalent queueing, concurrency, cancellation, copied-value or timing semantics. Bidirectional transformation and round-trip preservation are separate claims that require tests; neither is implemented by this release.

The revised assessment is therefore: **structural vocabulary and contract notation align more directly; bounded semantic transformation remains feasible with explicit constraints; general UML behavior/object transformation remains unsupported.** No adapter project or exchange-format implementation follows from this table.

<a id="source-2d2e1a9ea4f7-references"></a>

## References

- [OMG UML 2.5.1 specification and normative metamodels](https://www.omg.org/spec/UML/2.5.1/).
- [SWAML guide for UML architects](#source-386496547756), [structure and types](#source-4efedf58f92b), [communication](#source-410a83e3a0f0) and [behavior](#source-af5c925f5118).
- [Classes, operations and notation decision](#source-e41a024287b1).
